var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"location":"assets/examples/example-kkt-cholesky-regression/README/#KKT-Cholesky-regression-demo","page":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","title":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","text":"This example shows PerfTest.jl's LIKWID-backed regression detection catching a class of performance bug that no correctness test can ever see: a loop reordered so it fights Julia's memory layout instead of working with it.\n\nIt comes in two parts, each demonstrating a different performance-bug class that no correctness test can see:\n\nPart 1 (KktCholesky.jl + kkt_perftest.jl): a small, self-contained, hand-rolled Cholesky kernel — no dependency beyond PerfTest/LIKWID. The regression is a transposed loop order (same algorithm, worse memory access pattern).\nPart 2 (clarabel_perftest.jl): plugged directly into Clarabel.jl — a real interior-point QP/SOCP solver — by redefining one of its own internals at runtime. The regression is a disabled fill-reducing permutation (same Clarabel/QDLDL code end to end, worse elimination order).","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-kkt-cholesky-regression/README/#Background","page":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","title":"Background","text":"Interior-point methods for convex optimization — Clarabel.jl, OSQP, ECOS, and friends — spend nearly all of their time doing one thing over and over: factorizing and solving a symmetric \"KKT\" (or condensed normal-equations) linear system once per Newton iteration. Part 1 hand-rolls a kernel doing the same kind of work — build a synthetic SPD system K = AᵀA + σI, factorize it with Cholesky, solve by forward/back substitution — with no dependency on any real solver, so we have full control over its implementation and can inject a realistic regression into it. Part 2 takes that same idea — a performance bug invisible to correctness tests — and drives it through Clarabel.jl's actual factorization step instead of a synthetic stand-in, via a different kind of bug: a disabled fill-reducing permutation, rather than a transposed loop order.","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-kkt-cholesky-regression/README/#Part-1:-the-self-contained-kernel","page":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","title":"Part 1: the self-contained kernel","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-kkt-cholesky-regression/README/#The-kernel,-and-the-regression","page":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","title":"The kernel, and the regression","text":"Cholesky factorization has several textbook-equivalent loop orderings (kji, kij, jki, ... — see Golub & Van Loan). They all do the exact same arithmetic; they differ only in which array dimension the innermost loop walks. Julia (like Fortran and MATLAB) stores matrices column-major, so the innermost loop should walk down a column (stride 1) rather than across a row (stride n).\n\ncholesky_factor_fast! uses the kji order — column outer, row inner — which is the right choice for Julia:\n\nfor j in (k+1):n\n    ljk = L[j, k]\n    @simd for i in j:n\n        L[i, j] -= L[i, k] * ljk\n    end\nend\n\ncholesky_factor_regressed! is the same algorithm, updating the exact same set of matrix entries in the exact same order relative to the outer k loop — just with the inner two loops transposed (kij order, row outer, column inner):\n\nfor i in (k+1):n\n    lik = L[i, k]\n    @simd for j in (k+1):i\n        L[i, j] -= lik * L[j, k]\n    end\nend\n\nThis is exactly the kind of one-line-diff bug that shows up when porting or refactoring numerical code from a row-major reference implementation (C, NumPy) without adjusting for Julia's layout. Because each matrix entry is still updated by the same single subtraction in the same relative order, the two implementations produce bit-for-bit identical results — every @test you could write against the output will pass either way. Note also that @simd is still sitting right there on the regressed loop: the annotation alone doesn't fix a strided access pattern, which is exactly why you need hardware counters, not source inspection, to catch this.\n\nMeasured locally (Apple M-series, n=512 dense system — absolute numbers will differ on the real target machine, but the qualitative effect is a basic cache/memory-hierarchy phenomenon that reproduces on any CPU):\n\nimplementation time (median of 5)\ncholesky_factor_fast! ~6 ms\ncholesky_factor_regressed! ~130 ms (≈20×)","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-kkt-cholesky-regression/README/#The-recipe-(kkt_perftest.jl)","page":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","title":"The recipe (kkt_perftest.jl)","text":"using Test, PerfTest, LIKWID\nusing LinearAlgebra\n\ninclude(\"KktCholesky.jl\")\nusing .KktCholesky\n\nconst USE_FAST_FACTORIZATION = true   # <- the toggle you flip to \"introduce the regression\"\n\n@perftest_config \"\n[general]\nverbose = 3\n[regression]\nenabled = true\ndedicated_reference_file = 'kkt_reference.JLD2'\n\"\n\n@testset \"KKT Cholesky solve\" begin\n    n, m = 512, 256\n    K, b = build_kkt_system(n, m; seed=1)\n\n    @define_metric name=\"attained_gflops\" units=\"GFLOP/s\" begin\n        :flops / 1e9\n    end\n    @define_metric name=\"l3_miss_ratio\" units=\"ratio\" begin\n        :miss.l3\n    end\n    @define_metric name=\"mem_bandwidth\" units=\"MB/s\" begin\n        :bw.mem.read_write\n    end\n\n    @regression metrics=[:median_time, :l3_miss_ratio, :attained_gflops] threshold=[1.15, 1.20, 0.85] low_is_bad=[false, false, true]\n\n    x = @perftest samples=15 solve_kkt(K, b; fast=USE_FAST_FACTORIZATION)\n\n    @test relative_residual(K, b, x) < 1e-8\nend\n\nusing LIKWID activates PerfTest's PerfTest_LIKWIDExt extension. Any @perftest target in a testset that references LIKWID-backed formula symbols (:flop, :flops, :bw, :miss, :energy, :power — see ext/PerfTest_LIKWIDExt.jl for the full alias table) is automatically wrapped in LIKWID.perfmon with the right counter groups — there's no manual group selection.\n\n:flops reads LIKWID's FLOPS_DP group's precomputed rate metric (reported internally in raw FLOP/s, hence / 1e9 for GFLOP/s).\n:miss.l3 reads the L3CACHE group's \"L3 miss ratio\".\n:bw.mem.read_write reads the MEM group's \"Memory bandwidth [MBytes/s]\".\n\nThe @regression declaration tracks three metrics at once — wall time, attained GFLOP/s, and L3 miss ratio — so a failing run doesn't just say \"this got slower\", it tells you the mechanism: did the FLOP rate collapse, did the cache miss ratio spike, or both?","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-kkt-cholesky-regression/README/#Running-the-Part-1-demo","page":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","title":"Running the Part 1 demo","text":"julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nThen, from this directory:\n\nWith USE_FAST_FACTORIZATION = true (the shipped default), run:\njulia --project=. -e 'using PerfTest; runperftests(\"kkt_perftest.jl\")'\nFirst run: no baseline exists yet, everything passes, the baseline is recorded to kkt_reference.JLD2.\nRun the exact same command again — confirms stability (still compared against the run-1 baseline, still passes).\nEdit kkt_perftest.jl, flip USE_FAST_FACTORIZATION to false, rerun. The :median_time, l3_miss_ratio, and attained_gflops regression checks fail; the correctness @test still passes. Because this run wasn't fully green, kkt_reference.JLD2 is not updated — the original baseline persists.\nFlip USE_FAST_FACTORIZATION back to true and rerun — recovers, passes again against the same original baseline from step 1.\n\nOr run all four steps with ./run_demo.sh kkt_perftest.jl.\n\n(TODO: paste real console output and measured metric values here after running steps 1–4 on the target Linux/LIKWID machine.)\n\nOptionally, to see programmatically which metrics tripped a failing run (mirrors test/t5-regression.jl's style):\n\nusing PerfTest\nPerfTest.retrievePerfTests(\"kkt_reference.JLD2\"; get=:tests, where_pred=PerfTest.testFailed, pathonly=true)","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-kkt-cholesky-regression/README/#Part-2:-plugging-into-Clarabel.jl-directly","page":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","title":"Part 2: plugging into Clarabel.jl directly","text":"Part 1 proves the regression-detection idea works in isolation. Part 2 drives a different regression class through a real solver: Clarabel.jl, a pure-Julia interior-point solver for QPs/SOCPs/SDPs, used here as a dependency rather than reimplemented.","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-kkt-cholesky-regression/README/#The-extension-point,-and-the-redefinition","page":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","title":"The extension point, and the redefinition","text":"Clarabel's default direct linear-solve backend, QDLDLDirectLDLSolver, wraps QDLDL.jl, a sparse K = L D Lᵀ factorization for symmetric quasi-definite matrices — the structural property (positive-definite (1,1) block, negative-definite (2,2) block) that Clarabel's KKT/Newton systems always have, which guarantees an LDLᵀ factorization exists under any symmetric permutation, including the identity, without pivoting.\n\nQDLDL picks which permutation to use exactly once, at Clarabel.Solver(...) construction time, via its own qdldl(K; perm=..., ...) constructor. The default (perm left unspecified) computes a fill-reducing AMD ordering; passing perm=nothing is QDLDL's own documented \"factor without reordering\" mode — a first-class, publicly supported code path, not a hack. Every refactor!/solve! call afterward — one pair per Newton iteration, plus extra solve! calls for iterative refinement — is purely numeric and reuses whatever permutation was chosen at construction; it never revisits ordering.\n\nThis gives Part 2 a regression class that's arguably more representative than a transposed loop order: a disabled or broken fill-reducing permutation, a real, common sparse-solver performance bug. Crucially, it can be injected without touching refactor!/solve! at all, and without introducing any different algorithm — both USE_FAST_FACTORIZATION = true and false run Clarabel's real, unmodified QDLDL numeric code end to end; the only thing that differs is which permutation got baked in at construction:\n\nusing QDLDL\n\nfunction QDLDL._get_amd_ordering(A, amd_dense_scale)\n    if USE_FAST_FACTORIZATION\n        # Stock QDLDL behavior, verbatim.\n        meta = QDLDL.AMD.Amd()\n        meta.control[QDLDL.AMD.AMD_DENSE] *= Float64(amd_dense_scale)\n        return QDLDL.AMD.amd(A, meta)\n    else\n        return nothing\n    end\nend\n\n_get_amd_ordering(A, amd_dense_scale) is the private helper QDLDL uses to compute perm's default value. After using QDLDL, Julia lets this module add or replace methods on any generic function for any type it doesn't own — ordinary multiple dispatch, sometimes called \"monkey-patching\" (the same mechanism Part 2 used to rely on for overriding refactor!/solve! directly, before the regression moved to this construction-time hook). It's private (underscore-prefixed), not public API — re-verify its name and signature if the pinned QDLDL version (Project.toml pins QDLDL = \"0.4\" alongside Clarabel = \"0.11\") is ever bumped.\n\nWhy P had to become genuinely sparse. The naive version of this regression — just flip AMD off on whatever QP happens to be at hand — turns out to depend entirely on the problem: if the KKT matrix's dominant block is already fully dense, no permutation can avoid roughly the same O(n³) elimination cost either way, so disabling AMD barely registers. build_qp therefore builds P as a genuinely sparse SPD matrix — a random sparse graph (~6 edges/node) with diagonal dominance for guaranteed SPD-ness — and then randomly shuffles its variable labeling. AMD's ordering is derived purely from the sparsity graph, so it recovers a good elimination order regardless of labeling; the natural (post-shuffle) order has no such luck. That asymmetry — AMD is labeling-invariant, natural order isn't — is exactly what makes \"someone disabled AMD\" a dramatic, realistic regression on a sparse problem instead of a cosmetic one.\n\nVerified correctness: both branches converge Clarabel to SOLVED on the same QPs, to the same answer — they're the same algorithm and the same code, just under two different permutations of the same quasi-definite matrix, so (unlike Part 1's bit-for-bit identical loop-order variants) exact floating-point agreement isn't guaranteed, but both should agree well within Clarabel's own solver tolerance.\n\n(TODO: paste real fast-vs-regressed timing/GFLOP-s numbers here after running on the target Linux/LIKWID machine.)","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-kkt-cholesky-regression/README/#The-recipe-(clarabel_perftest.jl)","page":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","title":"The recipe (clarabel_perftest.jl)","text":"Same shape as Part 1 — inline @perftest_config, the same LIKWID-backed metrics, the same multi-metric @regression, the same USE_FAST_FACTORIZATION toggle — except the @perftest target constructs and solves a real Clarabel.Solver, and it sweeps n_vars over [32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048] instead of running a single fixed size:\n\nfunction solve_fresh_qp(P, q, A, b, cones, settings)\n    solver = Clarabel.Solver(P, q, A, b, cones, settings)\n    Clarabel.solve!(solver)\n    return solver\nend\n\nsolver = @perftest samples=5 evals=1 solve_fresh_qp(P, q, A, b, cones, settings)\n@test solver.solution.status == Clarabel.SOLVED\n\nConstructing a fresh solver inside the timed target (rather than via @perftest's setup=) keeps the target self-contained and measures \"cost of solving this QP\", not just \"cost of one factorization\" — the regression still shows up clearly since the bad permutation is paid for on every Newton iteration inside the same call.\n\nNote there's no intra-algorithm parallelism on either branch here — QDLDL is single-threaded by design, regardless of AMD ordering. @perftest_threads :all at the top of the file controls something different: how many Julia threads the perftest harness itself may use across samples, not anything inside Clarabel/QDLDL's own solve.","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-kkt-cholesky-regression/README/#Why-a-ratio-metric-like-l3_miss_ratio-wouldn't-be-the-right-catch-here-either","page":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","title":"Why a ratio metric like l3_miss_ratio wouldn't be the right catch here either","text":"l3_miss_ratio (:miss.l3) is a rate — L3 misses ÷ L3 references — not a count. A disabled fill-reducing permutation genuinely increases both the absolute amount of arithmetic (:flop — a worse elimination order creates more fill-in in L, so QDLDL does more real work, not just the same work laid out worse) and the memory traffic needed to do it, so a pure hit/miss ratio can end up roughly flat even as both numerator and denominator grow together — a ratio metric doesn't distinguish \"twice the work, same locality\" from \"the same work, half the locality.\"\n\nclarabel_perftest.jl instead defines an operational-intensity metric that normalizes L3 traffic by useful work directly:\n\n@define_metric name=\"l3_operational_intensity\" units=\"GFLOP/MB\" begin\n    (:flop / 1e9) / (:bw.l3.read_write * :median_time)\nend\n\n:bw.l3.read_write reads the L3 group's \"L3 bandwidth [MBytes/s]\" — MB/s of traffic at the L2/L3 boundary. Multiplying by :median_time turns that rate into total MB moved for one call; dividing GFLOP of useful work (:flop / 1e9) by that gives GFLOP done per MB of L3 traffic — the roofline-style \"operational intensity\" (flop/byte, see example-paper-pardiso), sourced from LIKWID counters instead of solver-printed stats. @regression tracks it with low_is_bad=false (a decrease is the failure signal): a disabled fill-reducing permutation should make QDLDL do measurably more work per byte of L3 traffic it moves, since the scattered fill-in it creates is exactly the kind of irregular, low-reuse access pattern a good ordering exists to avoid.","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-kkt-cholesky-regression/README/#Running-the-Part-2-demo","page":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","title":"Running the Part 2 demo","text":"Identical walkthrough to Part 1, just against the other recipe:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using PerfTest; runperftests(\"clarabel_perftest.jl\")'\n\nor ./run_demo.sh clarabel_perftest.jl for all four steps at once.\n\n(TODO: paste real console output and measured metric values here after running on the target Linux/LIKWID machine.)","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-kkt-cholesky-regression/README/#Requirements","page":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","title":"Requirements","text":"Linux only. LIKWID reads hardware performance counters via the MSR interface or the likwid-accessD daemon; it does not run on macOS. Both kernels' correctness can be authored and sanity-checked anywhere Julia runs (see below), but run the actual runperftests demos on the target Linux machine.\nLIKWID needs counter access: either run as root, have the msr kernel module loaded with appropriate permissions, or have likwid-accessD installed and running. This is a LIKWID/OS-level concern — PerfTest.jl has no configuration for it.\nPart 1's n = 512 dense system is sized to clear typical L2/L3 caches on the dev machine used above; if the regression doesn't show up clearly in l3_miss_ratio/mem_bandwidth on your target machine, scale up until the dense factor comfortably exceeds its L2/L3 size. Slowdown magnitude is also sensitive to whether the matrix dimension happens to alias badly against cache set size (e.g. powers of two are frequently pathological) — don't read too much into the exact multiplier, only into the fact that there reliably is one.\nPart 2's regression magnitude is driven by fill-in, not raw problem size: build_qp's sparse, randomly-relabeled P (see above) needs the natural-order factorization to actually generate substantially more fill-in than AMD's — controlled by avg_degree (graph density) as much as by n_vars. If the regression doesn't show up clearly in l3_operational_intensity/median_time on your target machine, or if the natural-order branch blows up disproportionately at the largest n_vars in the sweep, tune avg_degree and/or trim the sweep rather than assuming the current values are universally correct — they haven't been tuned against a specific target machine yet.\nPart 2 pins Clarabel = \"0.11\" and QDLDL = \"0.4\" in Project.toml: it redefines QDLDL's private _get_amd_ordering helper, which is not public API and can change name or signature across QDLDL releases. If you bump either pin, re-verify the override still matches before trusting the patched results.","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-kkt-cholesky-regression/README/#Running-these-recipes-via-bare-include()","page":"KKT Cholesky regression demo","title":"Running these recipes via bare include()","text":"Like example-quickstart's testfile.jl, both recipes can also be run as plain functional tests via include(\"kkt_perftest.jl\") / include(\"clarabel_perftest.jl\"), bypassing PerfTest.transform/ runperftests entirely (all PerfTest macros are no-ops outside the transform, so only the final @test actually runs, using whatever USE_FAST_FACTORIZATION is currently set to). This requires @perftest's keyword arguments to always precede the target expression, which is the last argument (@perftest samples=15 solve_kkt(...), not @perftest solve_kkt(...) samples=15) — this is also the order the real transform pipeline expects. KktCholesky.jl has no such restriction either way — it can be include()d and exercised directly for correctness checks independent of PerfTest. (KktLDLT.jl, the hand-rolled dense LDLᵀ kernel from an earlier version of Part 2's regression, is no longer used by clarabel_perftest.jl — Part 2's regression now lives entirely inside Clarabel/QDLDL's own code, see above — but the file is left in place unreferenced rather than deleted.)","category":"section"},{"location":"limitations/#Limitations-and-future-work:","page":"Limitations","title":"Limitations and future work:","text":"There are a couple of things to keep into consideration when using the package:\n\nGPU testing is technically supported, but it requires more effort from the developer to set up since the automatic measurements apart from time elapsed do not apply to GPUs (as of now).\nAutomatic flop counting is not supported (the :autoflop primitive is always 0), the flop count of a target has to be given explicitly, e.g. with @roofline actual_flops = ....\nLIKWID and GPU (CUDA/AMDGPU) energy/power measurements are repeated to match the actual number of samples BenchmarkTools took for the same test (median-aggregated, with the spread preserved as a standard-error-of-the-mean interval when showvar=true and at least 30 samples were taken — see @regression/@perfcompare's showvar argument), so their cost scales with whatever samples=/seconds=/evals= the test uses — tune those explicitly on tests using these extensions if you don't want extension sampling to inherit BenchmarkTools' adaptive defaults.","category":"section"},{"location":"limitations/#Features-to-be-expected-in-the-next-versions:","page":"Limitations","title":"Features to be expected in the next versions:","text":"Easier access to performance suite results after execution\nSimplification of the package structure, there will be an emphasis on making the package easy to extend for unfamiliarised developers\nAccess to performance counter values through LIKWID\nAlternative regression testing against git commits instead of last execution for easier testing","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/memcopyCellArray3D/#Basic-example:-time-regression","page":"Basic example: time regression","title":"Basic example: time regression","text":"Given a very simple function, which can be replaced by any other the user might want, we will build a time regression performance test suite.\n\nfunction testfun(a :: Int)\n    c = 1\n\n    for i in 1:a\n        c = c + (i ^ 2) / c\n    end\n\n    return c\nend\n\nThe source file for the test suite would then be:\n\nusing PerfTest\n\n@testset \"Test unit\" for i in [10000000,20000000,30000000]\n    x = @perftest testfun(10)\nend\n\nThis source specifies that the function shall be tested for the 3 different values on the test loop. The methodology used is time regression, this methodology will compare the median time attained with an historical reference and fail if outside the default tolerance interval.","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/roofline/#Roofline-example:-option-pricing","page":"Roofline example: option pricing","title":"Roofline example: option pricing","text":"In this example we setup a roofline model methodology to test the resolution of a binomial tree using a slow implementation.\n\nrfd_rt(r,t_per_p) = rfd(t) = exp(-r * t * t_per_p)\nup_st(sigma, t_per_p) = exp(sigma * sqrt(t_per_p))\n\nstruct _Parameters\n    sigma   :: Float64\n    periods :: Int\n    t_per_p :: Float64\n    rfd     :: Function\n    up      :: Float64\n    K       :: Float64\n    S0      :: Float64\n    p       :: Float64\nend\n\nfunction setupProblem(sigma, t, periods_per_t, risk_free_rate, strike, price, p) :: _Parameters\n    return _Parameters(\n        sigma,\n        t * periods_per_t,\n        1 / periods_per_t,\n        rfd_rt(risk_free_rate, 1 / periods_per_t),\n        up_st(sigma, 1 / periods_per_t),\n        strike,\n        price,\n        p\n    )\nend\n\nfunction createBinomialTree(p :: _Parameters)::Tuple{Matrix,Array}\n\n    nodes = (p.periods + 1) * p.periods ÷ 2\n    # A and b\n    Tree = zeros(Float64, nodes, nodes)\n    B = zeros(Float64, nodes)\n\n    # Fill the body of the tree with the discounted expectations\n    for period in 1:(p.periods-1)\n        _nodes = ((period-1)*period÷2+1):((period+1)*(period+0)÷2)\n        for node in _nodes\n            Tree[node, node] = -1\n            Tree[node, node+period] = p.p * p.rfd(p.periods - period)\n            Tree[node, node+period+1] = 1 - p.p * p.rfd(p.periods - period)\n        end\n    end\n\n    # Fill the last level of the tree with the prices at maturity\n    xa = ((p.periods - 1) * p.periods ÷ 2 + 1)\n    xb = ((p.periods + 1) * (p.periods + 0) ÷ 2)\n    down = 1 / p.up\n\n    for node in xa:xb\n        Tree[node, node] = 1\n        B[node] = max((p.S0 * p.up^(xb - node) * down^(node - xa)) - p.K, 0.0)\n    end\n    return Tree, B\nend\n\n\nfunction solveTree(A::Matrix, b::Array, periods::Int)::Array\n\n    nodes = (periods + 1) * periods ÷ 2\n    X = zeros(Float64, nodes)\n    # Last level nodes from a to b\n    xa = ((periods - 1) * periods ÷ 2 + 1)\n    xb = ((periods + 1) * (periods + 0) ÷ 2)\n    for node in xa:xb\n        X[node] = b[node]\n    end\n    # Tree core nodes\n    for period in (periods-1):-1:1\n        _nodes = ((period-1)*period÷2+1):((period+1)*(period+0)÷2)\n        for node in _nodes\n            X[node] = A[node, node+period] * b[node+period] + A[node, node+period+1] * b[node+period+1]\n            b[node] = X[node]\n        end\n    end\n\n    return X\nend\n\noption_price(solution :: Array) = solution[1]\n\n\nThe following is the recipe file that sets up the performance test suite:\n\nusing Test\nusing PerfTest\n\ninclude(\"main3.jl\")\n\n# CONFIG\n@perftest_config begin\n    regression.enabled = false\n    roofline.autoflops = true\n    roofline.tolerance.min_percentage = 1.0\nend\n\n\n@testset \"Performance Tests\" begin\n\n    @testset \"Tree construction\" begin\n\n        @testset \"Periods\" for n_periods in [16, 64, 128]\n\n            param = setupProblem(0.04, 1, n_periods, 0.04, 100, 100, 0.5)\n\n            @roofline actual_flops=:autoflop target_ratio=0.05 begin\n                mem = ((:iterator + 1) * :iterator)\n                :autoflop / mem\n            end\n            A, b = createBinomialTree(param)\n\n\n            @perftest solveTree(A, b, param.periods)\n        end\n    end\nend\n\nThe recipe file specifies the following:\n\nThe target will be evaluated using a roofline model\nThe basic regression methodology is disabled\nThe tolerance below the threshold will be zero\nThe autoflops feature is enabled\nThe roofline macro additionally receives how to calculate the flop count (actual_lops=:autoflop) and the threshold of the actual performance vs model performance is 5%","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/basic/#Basic-example:-time-regression","page":"Basic example: time regression","title":"Basic example: time regression","text":"Given a very simple function, which can be replaced by any other the user might want, we will build a time regression performance test suite.\n\nfunction testfun(a :: Int)\n    c = 1\n\n    for i in 1:a\n        c = c + (i ^ 2) / c\n    end\n\n    return c\nend\n\nThe source file for the test suite would then be:\n\nusing PerfTest\n\n@testset \"Test unit\" for i in [10000000,20000000,30000000]\n    x = @perftest testfun(10)\nend\n\nThis source specifies that the function shall be tested for the 3 different values on the test loop. The methodology used is time regression, this methodology will compare the median time attained with an historical reference and fail if outside the default tolerance interval.","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-llama-energy/README/#Case-study-—-Energy-efficiency-of-LLM-inference-vs.-quantization","page":"Case study — Energy efficiency of LLM inference vs. quantization","title":"Case study — Energy efficiency of LLM inference vs. quantization","text":"Status: preliminary draft. This directory is a self-contained scaffold for a PerfTest.jl case study. The Julia recipe, the FFI binding, and the helper scripts are written to be runnable in shape, but the FFI struct layouts and the exact numbers must be validated on the target machine (see Caveats).","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-llama-energy/README/#The-question","page":"Case study — Energy efficiency of LLM inference vs. quantization","title":"The question","text":"Almost every published llama.cpp benchmark reports tokens per second. For production inference at scale, the metric that actually drives cost is energy per generated token (Joules/token). Quantization is the main lever for reducing it — but it also degrades output quality.\n\nThis case study uses PerfTest.jl's new CUDA/NVML energy feature to answer:\n\nHow much energy does quantization save, and what does it cost in quality?\n\nThe deliverable is a Pareto plot: energy-per-token (x) vs. quality loss vs. the F16 baseline (y), one point per quantization level (F16 → Q8_0 → Q6_K → Q5_K_M → Q4_K_M → Q3_K_M → Q2_K).","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-llama-energy/README/#Why-this-is-a-good-demonstration-of-PerfTest.jl","page":"Case study — Energy efficiency of LLM inference vs. quantization","title":"Why this is a good demonstration of PerfTest.jl","text":"It measures a real, non-Julia GPU workload (llama.cpp's own CUDA context) entirely from a Julia test recipe. This works because NVML's energy_consumption is a device-global counter — PerfTest.jl (on CUDA.jl) reads it around each target, capturing whatever the GPU did, regardless of which CUDA context ran it.\nInference is driven in-process via direct ccalls into libllama (LlamaFFI.jl), so prefill and decode can be bracketed separately and the energy attributed cleanly, without subprocess or model-load contamination.\nThe measured targets are idempotent by construction (they reset the KV cache and re-prefill on every call), so repeated benchmark samples and the separate energy probe all start from an identical state — see Idempotency.\nIt exercises PerfTest.jl idioms end-to-end: loop testsets, @perftest, @export_vars, custom/auxiliary metrics, a testable energy budget (@define_test_metric), and persisted results.","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-llama-energy/README/#Files","page":"Case study — Energy efficiency of LLM inference vs. quantization","title":"Files","text":"File Purpose\nllama_energy_test.jl The PerfTest.jl recipe (quant sweep + energy metrics).\nLlamaFFI.jl Minimal in-process ccall binding to a CUDA libllama.\nbuild_llama_cuda.sh Build llama.cpp with -DGGML_CUDA=ON.\nget_models.sh Download or quantize the GGUF weights.\nplot_results.jl Produce the energy/quality Pareto figure.\nProject.toml Deps (PerfTest, CUDA; optional plotting).","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-llama-energy/README/#Requirements","page":"Case study — Energy efficiency of LLM inference vs. quantization","title":"Requirements","text":"A datacenter-class NVIDIA GPU (V100 / A100 / H100 …). NVML nvmlDeviceGetTotalEnergyConsumption is supported on Volta+ datacenter cards; many consumer GeForce cards do not expose the cumulative energy counter.\nA CUDA build of llama.cpp. The registered llama_cpp_jll is CPU-only (no libggml_cuda), so it cannot be used here. build_llama_cuda.sh also builds the llama-perplexity tool used for the quality axis (see below).\nA fixed text corpus for the quality axis (e.g. a WikiText-2 slice) — see \"Quality axis\" for how to fetch one.\nA checkout of PerfTest.jl with the CUDA extension enabled.\nDisk/VRAM scale. The default model, Qwen3.8-27B, ranges from ~26GB (Q2_K) to ~141GB (f16) per quant. The larger quants (f16/Q8_0/ Q6_K, and possibly Q5_K_M) will not fit in a single GPU's VRAM on most datacenter cards at n_ctx=4096 — that's expected, see \"Caveats: OOM behavior\" below. get_models.sh's split-file fallback (needed because those larger quants are published as multiple part files, not one) needs only curl/grep plus the llama-gguf-split binary already built alongside libllama by build_llama_cuda.sh/build_llama_rocm.sh.","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-llama-energy/README/#Reproduction","page":"Case study — Energy efficiency of LLM inference vs. quantization","title":"Reproduction","text":"# 1. Build CUDA-enabled llama.cpp (set CUDA_ARCH: 70=V100, 80=A100, 90=H100)\nCUDA_ARCH=80 ./build_llama_cuda.sh\nexport LLAMA_CPP_LIB=/abs/path/to/llama.cpp/build/bin/libllama.so\n\n# 2. Get the weights (A = download prebuilt, B = quantize from an F16 base)\n#    and the WikiText-2 corpus used for the quality axis.\nexport LLAMA_MODEL_DIR=$PWD/models\nexport LLAMA_MODEL_BASE=Qwen3.8-27B\n# LLAMA_MODEL_REPO is also available (defaults to\n# unsloth/${LLAMA_MODEL_BASE}-GGUF) for the next time the model changes.\n./get_models.sh A\n\n# 3. (Recommended) stabilise the GPU for repeatable energy numbers\nsudo nvidia-smi -pm 1              # persistence mode\nsudo nvidia-smi -lgc <min>,<max>  # lock graphics clocks\n\n# 4. Run the study\njulia --project=. -e 'using PerfTest; runperftests(\"llama_energy_test.jl\")'\n\nTo generate the Pareto figure, populate results.csv (see plot_results.jl header for the expected columns) and:\n\njulia --project=. plot_results.jl","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-llama-energy/README/#Experimental-design","page":"Case study — Energy efficiency of LLM inference vs. quantization","title":"Experimental design","text":"Aspect Choice\nControlled one model family, one GPU, full GPU offload, fixed prompt, fixed N_GEN, greedy (deterministic) decode, fixed flash-attention, locked clocks\nIndependent variable GGUF quantization level (loop testset)\nPrimary metric Joules per generated token (decode_energy / N_GEN)\nSecondary tokens/Joule, avg power (W), throughput (tok/s), VRAM\nQuality axis perplexity (or KL-divergence) vs the F16 baseline","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-llama-energy/README/#Quality-axis","page":"Case study — Energy efficiency of LLM inference vs. quantization","title":"Quality axis","text":"Two ways to obtain perplexity were considered:\n\nLevel A (offline, used by the recipe): shell out to the CUDA-built llama-perplexity tool once per quant against a fixed corpus (e.g. a WikiText-2 slice), and parse its Final estimate: PPL = ... line. llama_energy_test.jl's run_llama_perplexity does this in setup, before the in-process LlamaFFI session opens (so the two CUDA contexts never share the GPU at once), and reports the result via the Perplexity auxiliary metric.\nLevel B (in-process, dropped): teacher-force text through LlamaFFI directly and exponentiate the average negative log-likelihood of the gold next-tokens. This was tried first, using the ~30-token PROMPT as the eval text, and produced non-monotonic, noisy perplexity across quants (e.g. some quantized levels scoring better than F16). A sample that small can't average out per-token noise from quantization — real perplexity evaluation needs a corpus of thousands of tokens, which is exactly what llama-perplexity's sliding-window evaluation provides. Level A was kept instead of reimplementing that chunking in LlamaFFI.jl.\n\nget_models.sh fetches the corpus automatically (fetch_ppl_corpus, run regardless of the A/B model option). The HF dataset repo now ships that split as Parquet only (the old .raw/.zip files are gone — wikitext-2-raw-v1), so fetch_ppl_corpus downloads test-00000-of-00001.parquet and reassembles it into a plain-text file (one physical line per row) using Julia + Parquet2.jl in a throwaway temp environment — it doesn't touch Project.toml/Manifest.toml. To fetch it standalone:\n\nmkdir -p wikitext-2-raw\ncurl -L -o wikitext-2-raw/test-00000-of-00001.parquet \\\n    https://huggingface.co/datasets/Salesforce/wikitext/resolve/main/wikitext-2-raw-v1/test-00000-of-00001.parquet\njulia --startup-file=no -e '\n    import Pkg; Pkg.activate(; temp=true); Pkg.add([\"Parquet2\", \"Tables\"])\n    using Parquet2, Tables\n    ds = Parquet2.Dataset(ARGS[1])\n    open(ARGS[2], \"w\") do io\n        for row in Tables.rows(ds)\n            text = coalesce(row.text, \"\")\n            print(io, text)\n            endswith(text, \"\\n\") || print(io, \"\\n\")\n        end\n    end\n' wikitext-2-raw/test-00000-of-00001.parquet wikitext-2-raw/wiki.test.raw\n\nrun_llama_perplexity looks for llama-perplexity next to $LLAMA_LIB (override with LLAMA_PERPLEXITY_BIN) and the corpus at wikitext-2-raw/wiki.test.raw next to this recipe (override with PPL_CORPUS). If either is missing it @warns and reports NaN rather than failing the sweep.","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-llama-energy/README/#How-the-recipe-measures-energy","page":"Case study — Energy efficiency of LLM inference vs. quantization","title":"How the recipe measures energy","text":"The CUDA extension injects an NVML read before and after each @perftest target (bracketed by a synchronize), storing the per-device delta. In the recipe:\n\n@perftest ... LlamaFFI.generate!(s, PROMPT, N_GEN) is the measured target (idempotent full request); its GPU energy is exposed to formulas as :gpue.dev0 (Joules) and average power as :gpup.dev0 (Watts).\n@auxiliary_metric name=\"Energy/token\" ... :gpue.dev0 / N_GEN is the headline.\n@define_test_metric name=\"Energy budget\" reference=0.5 low_is_bad=false ... demonstrates a testable energy target (fail the build if J/token exceeds the budget), which is the basis for energy-regression testing in CI.","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-llama-energy/README/#Idempotency-(why-the-measured-target-self-resets)","page":"Case study — Energy efficiency of LLM inference vs. quantization","title":"Idempotency (why the measured target self-resets)","text":"LLM decoding is stateful: each llama_decode appends to the context KV cache. A naive decode_n! target is therefore not idempotent — the 2nd benchmark sample would continue from the 1st sample's state and eventually overflow n_ctx.\n\nYou might reach for @perftest setup=... teardown=... to reset between runs. That is not sufficient here, for two reasons specific to the current CUDA energy extension:\n\nThe energy probe runs the target once more, with no setup/teardown (hierarchy_transform.jl injects gpuPowerMeasure(expr) directly), so a BenchmarkTools setup never runs for the energy measurement.\nThat energy run happens after the timing loop, so it would start from the most polluted state of all.\n\nThe robust fix used here is to make the measured targets self-resetting: generate! and prefill_only! call reset! (clear the KV cache) at the start of every invocation, so they are safe to call any number of times and always start from an identical state — no setup/teardown required. If the extension later gains setup/teardown support around the energy probe, the reset could move there instead.","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-llama-energy/README/#Caveats-(must-read-for-the-draft)","page":"Case study — Energy efficiency of LLM inference vs. quantization","title":"Caveats (must-read for the draft)","text":"FFI struct layouts. LlamaModelParams / LlamaContextParams in LlamaFFI.jl mirror the C structs so the *_default_params() calls work. These layouts change across llama.cpp commits — verify them against the llama.h of the commit you build, or regenerate the binding with Clang.jl. Pin and record the commit.\nEnergy-feature prototype. The PerfTest CUDA extension is still a prototype. Before trusting numbers, the known blockers should be fixed: unit conversion (NVML returns millijoules), real power (currently aliased to energy), honouring the .devN device specifier, gating the CUDA branch properly, and removing debug @info logs. See the project plan / issue tracker.\nSingle-run energy. Energy is measured on one execution of the target (separate from the timing samples). Use warmup + repetitions and report mean ± std for credibility.\nWhole-device counter. NVML energy is device-global; run on an otherwise idle GPU, and consider subtracting an idle-power baseline.\nDeterminism. Greedy decoding is used so token counts and work are identical across repetitions; keep sampling out of the measured path.\nOOM behavior. Neither LlamaFFI.jl nor PerfTest.jl's testset machinery has any OOM-specific handling, and at 70B some quants (see \"Disk/VRAM scale\" above) are expected to exceed a single GPU's VRAM. Concretely:\nA failed cudaMalloc/hipMalloc inside llama.cpp's GPU backend either returns an error/null pointer, or calls GGML_ABORT/segfaults — which one happens depends on the llama.cpp commit/backend, not on anything in this example. In the first case, LlamaFFI.jl's generic ... == C_NULL && error(...) checks turn it into a normal Julia exception, which @testset for records as an Error for that one quant and the sweep continues. In the second case, the crash cannot be caught by any Julia try/catch — the OS kills the whole process mid-ccall.\nThe generated perf-suite always runs as a separate subprocess (Launch.launchPerfTestSuite, spawned via run(...)), with no surrounding try/catch in runperftests. A hard crash there surfaces as an uncaught Base.ProcessFailedException out of runperftests — visible, but not gracefully handled (no retry, no partial results).\nResults are saved once, after the entire quant sweep finishes. QUANTS sweeps smallest → largest specifically so the quants most likely to fit are computed before the large, VRAM-constrained ones — but this is only a partial mitigation: if a large quant crashes hard (rather than just erroring), the process dies before that end-of-run save executes, so the smaller quants' results, though already computed, are still lost along with everything else. Full protection would need incremental per-quant saving, which this recipe does not do.","category":"section"},{"location":"assets/examples/example-llama-energy/README/#Open-questions-being-finalised","page":"Case study — Energy efficiency of LLM inference vs. quantization","title":"Open questions being finalised","text":"~~Model choice (Qwen2.5-7B, Apache-2.0 vs Llama-3.1-8B) and quant set.~~ Resolved: Qwen3.8-27B, chosen for sharper energy/quality separation across the quant sweep and to realistically exercise the OOM path (see \"Caveats: OOM behavior\").\nWhether to also demo energy-regression testing in CI.","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/mock3-roofline/#Roofline","page":"Roofline","title":"Roofline","text":"This mock example explains how to setup the effective roofline methodology.\n\nusing Test\nusing PerfTest\n\n# Disable regression enable verbosity to see successful tests\n@perftest_config \"\n[regression]\nenabled = false\n[general]\nverbose = true\n\"\n\n\n# TEST TARGET\nfunction polynom(x :: Float64, coeff :: Vector{Float64})\n\n    res = 0.\n\n    for i in length(coeff):-1:1\n        res += coeff[i] * x ^ i\n    end\n\n    return res\nend\n\n@testset \"Example\" begin\n\n    N = 50\n\n    coeff = rand(N)\n    x = 1.0\n\n    @roofline actual_flops=:autoflop target_ratio=0.1 begin\n        :autoflop / ((1 + N)*4)\n    end\n\n    # Set the target\n    res = @perftest polynom(x, coeff)\nend\n\nThis test results in a success to meet the expectation.","category":"section"},{"location":"internals/#Translation-ruleset-and-priority","page":"Internals","title":"Translation ruleset and priority","text":"PerfTest.jl translates a declarative recipe file for performance tests into a Julia expression that can actually execute them. The traslation prodedure is done by walking through the recipe code whilst trying to do pattern matching with a set of translation rules. The translation rules are totally ordered, which means that there is a clear hierarchy on which pattern is translated in case an expression fits two patterns at the same time. Below there is a full list of the rules, in decreasing order by priority. Given the way the macros in the package are declared there should not be ambiguous pattern matching but for further safety the priority is enforced.\n\n@testset macro\n@test macro\n@test_throws macro\n@test_logs macro\n@inferred macro\n@test_deprecated macro\n@test_warn macro\n@test_nowarn macro\n@test_broken macro\n@test_skip macro\n@perftest macro\nBack context token, internal use only\nPrefix placement token, internal use only\nSuffix placement token, internal use only\n@config macro\n@on_perftest_exec macro\n@on_perftest_ignore macro\n@define_eff_memory_throughput macro\n@define_metric macro\n@define_benchmark macro\n@roofline macro\n@auxiliary_metric macro \n@perfcompare macro\nRecursivity rule, used for includes inside testsets\n\nInside a @perftest macro the expression has to be manipulated to be able tu use local variables in a global context (a requirement of BenchmarkTools.jl). The rules have the following priority:\n\nAssignment pattern rule:  a = b\nFunction argument pattern rule:  f(arg)\nVectorized function call pattern rule: f.(arg)\nDot operator pattern rule: a.b","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","title":"API reference","text":"This is the official API reference of PerfTest. Note that it can also be queried interactively from the Julia REPL using the help mode:\n\njulia> using PerfTest\njulia>?\nhelp?> PerfTest","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Types","page":"API reference","title":"Types","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Index","page":"API reference","title":"Index","text":"Order = [:type]","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Documentation","page":"API reference","title":"Documentation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Functions","page":"API reference","title":"Functions","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Index-2","page":"API reference","title":"Index","text":"Order = [:function, :macro]","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#Documentation-2","page":"API reference","title":"Documentation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.ASTRule","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.ASTRule","text":"Used by the AST walker to check for expressions that match condition, if they do then modifier will be applied to the expression.\n\nThis is the basic building block of the code transformer, a set of these rules compounds to all the needed manipulations to create the testing suite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Context","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Context","text":"In order to perform with the test suite generation, the AST walk needs to keep a context register to integrate features that rely on the scope hierarchy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.MacroParameter-Tuple{Symbol, Type}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.MacroParameter","text":"MacroParameter(name::Symbol, type::Type;\n              validation_function::Function=(_...) -> true,\n              default_value=nothing,\n              has_default::Bool=false,\n              mandatory::Bool=false)\n\nDefine a parameter that can appear in a macro along with properties to validate values when the macro is parsed.\n\nArguments\n\nname: The parameter name as a symbol\ntype: The expected type of the parameter\nvalidation_function: Optional function (returns Bool) to validate parameter values\ndefault_value: Optional default value for the parameter\nhas_default: Whether this parameter has a default value\nmandatory: Whether this parameter is required\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Methodology_Result","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Methodology_Result","text":"This struct is used in the test suite to save a methodology result, which in turn is constituted of a group of metric results and their references. Additionally, custom elements that are not subject to test are also saved, e.g. informational metrics, printing functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Metric_Result","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Metric_Result","text":"This struct is used in the test suite to save a metric measurement, therefore its saves the metric name, its units space and its value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Metric_Test","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Metric_Test","text":"This struct is used in the test suite to save a metric test result and its associated data, it saves the reference used and the toreance intervals in absolute and percentual values, also it shows if the test succeded and some additional variables for data printing\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Perftest_Datafile_Root","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Perftest_Datafile_Root","text":"This struct is the root of the data recording file, it can save several performance test suite execution results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.SampledValue","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.SampledValue","text":"A scalar value paired with the standard error of the mean (sem) and the raw sample count (n) that backed it. Arithmetic on SampledValues propagates sem through ordinary Julia operator dispatch (absolute errors add in quadrature for +/-, relative errors add in quadrature for */÷), so formulas built from transformFormula/SBMID (which splice plain Julia arithmetic around resolved symbols) get variance propagation without any changes to the AST-rewriting machinery itself. A plain Real promotes to a zero-variance SampledValue with n = typemax(Int), so it never becomes the bottleneck of a min(a.n, b.n) combination.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Suite_Execution_Result","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Suite_Execution_Result","text":"This struct saves a complete test suite result for one execution. It also saves the raw measurements obtained from the targets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Test_Result","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Test_Result","text":"NOTE: SOME METRICS ARE REPEATED IN HERE AND INSIDE A METRIC RESULT, this redundancy is tolerated for now, the copy inside the methodology result might be substituted by a reference in the future.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.SBMID-Tuple{Val{:LIKWID}, Vector{Symbol}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.SBMID","text":"Native LIKWID names, :LIKWID.METRICS.<group>.<metric> or :LIKWID.EVENTS.<group>.<event> (e.g. :LIKWID.METRICS.FLOPS_DP.var\"DP [MFLOP/s]\"), summed over the threads as the aliases of the LIKWID extension are\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest._perftest_config-Tuple{String}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest._perftest_config","text":"Used on a generated test suite to make, while it executes, the configuration changes made during its generation:   the whole configuration it was generated with at its beginning, then each @perftest_config where it appears.   An entry with an incorrect type, which the transform reports as a parse error so only a suite generated by a different   version of the package can have, is reported as an error and ignored, the rest still apply (see merge_configs)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest._treeRun-Tuple{Expr, PerfTest.Context, Vararg{Any}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest._treeRun","text":"This method gets a input julia expression, and a context register and executes a transformation of the input that converts a recipe script (input) into a fully-fledged testing suite (return value).\n\nArguments\n\ninput_expr the recipe/source expression. (internally, a.k.a source code space)\ncontext a register that will store information useful for the transformation over its run over the AST of the input\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest._walk_perftests","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest._walk_perftests","text":"_walk_perftests(f, perftests, path=String[])\n\nRecursively walk a perftests dict, calling f(test, path) for every Test_Result leaf, where path is the vector of testset names leading to it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.abort_if_invalid-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.abort_if_invalid","text":"If validation failed with a false, the transform will abort returning an empty expression.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.addLog","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.addLog","text":"Creates and/or appends to a log channel, the message is saved in that channel   depending on verbosity the message will be also sent to standard output.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.auxiliarMetricPrint-Tuple{PerfTest.Metric_Result, Int64}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.auxiliarMetricPrint","text":"This function is used to dump metric information regading auxiliar metrics, which are not used in testing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.buildMemTRPTMethodology-Tuple{PerfTest.Context}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.buildMemTRPTMethodology","text":"Returns an expression used to evaluate the effective memory throughput over a test target\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.buildRegression-Tuple{PerfTest.Context}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.buildRegression","text":"Returns an expression used to evaluate regression over a test target\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.buildRoofline-Tuple{PerfTest.Context}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.buildRoofline","text":"Returns an expression used to generate and evaluate a roofline model over a test target\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.by_index-Tuple{Union{BenchmarkTools.BenchmarkGroup, Dict}, Vector{PerfTest.DepthRecord}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.by_index","text":"This method expects a hierarchy tree (dict) in the form of nested dictionaries and a vector of dictionary keys idx. The function will recursively index by the keys to get to a final element.\n\nThe DepthRecord struct represents an index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.checkMetricSymbol!-Tuple{AbstractString, Any, PerfTest.Context}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.checkMetricSymbol!","text":"Report as a parse error that construct (e.g. \"The @definemetric name = \"m\"\"), of the recipe being transformed, sets a symbol that formulas cannot refer to: an empty one, or a built-in formula symbol (e.g. :mediantime), which formulas resolve to the built-in value instead. Only an explicit symbol is checked, the default one (the name) is left as it was.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.checkType-Tuple{Type}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.checkType","text":"Returns a function that will return if its argument (x) is of type \"type\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.clearLogs","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.clearLogs","text":"Clears the IOBuffer of the specified channel   if 0 all channels will be cleared.\n\nUnbinds the channels to stdout as well.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.defaultRegressionThresholds-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any, PerfTest.Context}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.defaultRegressionThresholds","text":"Thresholds of a @regression that gives none, one for each of its metrics from regression.defaultthreshold: the threshold itself for a metric where a high value is bad (lowisbad = false, e.g. a time), and 2 − threshold for one where a low value is bad (lowis_bad = true, e.g. a throughput). The default 1.1 thus tolerates a time up to 10% higher, and a throughput up to 10% lower (0.9), than the reference. 2 − threshold is computed on the decimal value it is written with, so 1.1 gives exactly 0.9.\n\nA regression.default_threshold of 2 or more leaves no threshold for a metric where a low value is bad, it is reported as a parse error naming where it comes from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.deviceMedians-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Real}}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.deviceMedians","text":"One value per device from samples, which holds the value of every device for each sample: the median of the samples of each device, a SampledValue when there are enough of them for its variance. Used by the GPU extensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.dumpLogs","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.dumpLogs","text":"Prints the selected channel number on stdout\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.dumpLogsString","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.dumpLogsString","text":"Prints the selected channel number on a string\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.empty_expr","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.empty_expr","text":"Returns an empty expresion for any input\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.enableLikwidGroup!","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.enableLikwidGroup!","text":"Enables the LIKWID group group for the tests of the testset being transformed and of its inner testsets, as its metrics apply to them. A formula that reads a LIKWID metric or event calls it, so that group is measured.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.enabledLikwidGroups-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.enabledLikwidGroups","text":"The LIKWID groups measured for a test, the ones enabled in its testset and in the testsets around it, sorted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.extractMethodologyResultArray-Tuple{Dict, Symbol}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.extractMethodologyResultArray","text":"This method will return a flattened array of all of the results for all the methodologies exercised in the provided dictionary.\n\nExample:\n\n\"Test Set 1\"      -> \"Test 1\"          -> Methodology A result          -> Methodology B result  \"Test Set 2\"      -> \"Test 1\"          -> Methodology A result Returns:  M. A result (Test Set 1)  M. B result (Test Set 1)  M. A result (Test Set 2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.extractNamesResultArray-Tuple{Dict, Symbol}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.extractNamesResultArray","text":"This method will return a flattened array of the whole test result hierarchy.\n\nExample\n\nExample:\n\n\"Test Set 1\"      -> \"Test 1\"          -> Methodology A result          -> Methodology B result  \"Test Set 2\"      -> \"Test 1\"          -> Methodology A result Returns:  \"Test Set 1 -> Test 1 -> Methodology A\"  \"Test Set 1 -> Test 1 -> Methodology B\"  \"Test Set 2 -> Test 1 -> Methodology A\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.flattenedInterpolation-Tuple{Expr, Expr, Symbol}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.flattenedInterpolation","text":"This method interpolates the inside_expr into outside_expr anywhere it finds the token substitution_token, which is a symbol. The outside_expr has to be a block or a quote block. It has the particularity that it will remove block heads from the inside_expr and add the nested elements onto the location where the token it.\n\nExample:\n\noutside_expr = :(:A; 4)\n\ninside_expr = :(begin 2;3 end)\n\nsubstitution_token = :A\n\nreturns = :(2;3;4)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.genTestName!-Tuple{PerfTest.Context}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.genTestName!","text":"Function that generates a test name if needed, it is used to name   test targets to distinguish them if several go in the same testset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.getExecutionTimestamps-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.getExecutionTimestamps","text":"getExecutionTimestamps(datafile_path::AbstractString)\n\nReturns a vector of timestamps corresponding to each execution recorded in the datafile at `datafile_path`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.getMetricValue-Tuple{Vector{PerfTest.Methodology_Result}, String}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.getMetricValue","text":"Given a series of methodology results, the the raw values of all the metrics contained in the methodology results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.getNumber-Tuple{String}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.getNumber","text":"From a string (field), it will parse the first number it finds as a Float\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.get_failed_tests-Tuple{PerfTest.Perftest_Datafile_Root}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.get_failed_tests","text":"This function will return all tests that failed (i.e., where succeeded is false).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.get_metric_results-Tuple{PerfTest.Perftest_Datafile_Root, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.get_metric_results","text":"This function will return all results for a specific metric across all methodologies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.get_metrics_for_test-Tuple{PerfTest.Perftest_Datafile_Root, AbstractString}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.get_metrics_for_test","text":"This function will return all metrics for a specific test name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.grepOutput-Tuple{String, Union{Regex, String}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.grepOutput","text":"From a string, it will divide it by lines and retrieve the ones that match the regular expression provided.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.grepOutputXGetNumber-Tuple{String, String}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.grepOutputXGetNumber","text":"Given a string output, it will retrieve the first number in the first line that contains the string string.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.isAlias-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.isAlias","text":"Whether sym is an alias with a formula of its own (e.g. :time, or :flop when the LIKWID extension is loaded), and not left to the fallback of newSymbols, which reports it as unrecognized\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.isThreadsLiteral-Tuple{Union{AbstractString, Number}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.isThreadsLiteral","text":"Whether x, the argument of a @perftest_threads, is a literal, so its value is known when the recipe is transformed: a number, a string, a quoted symbol (e.g. :numa), a ratio of integers (e.g. 1//2), or a tuple or a list of them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.loadFileAsExpr-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.loadFileAsExpr","text":"Utility to get an expression from a Julia file stored at path\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.loadTransformConfig","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.loadTransformConfig","text":"Load the configuration a top level transform starts from, and make it the current one: the configuration file, overridden by config (e.g. the config argument of runperftests, source names it in the reported errors), overridden by overrides (overrides_source names them). The configuration file is loaded, and its errors reported, once. A top level transform never inherits the configuration of a previous one. Where each value comes from is recorded in Configuration.SOURCES.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.match_failure-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.match_failure","text":"Constructs an ASTRule that will always match. The onfail function will then be applied.   Useful to catch a match failure.\n\nArguments:\n\nonfail : a function that receives a Context argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.metaGet-Tuple{AbstractVector, Symbol}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.metaGet","text":"Runs over an array of expressions trying to match the desired one.   If not found returns \"Nothing\".\n\n\"sym\" should follow the MacroTools nomenclature for the @capture macro\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.metaGetString-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.metaGetString","text":"\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.metricSampledValue-Tuple{PerfTest.Metric_Result}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.metricSampledValue","text":"Reconstructs a SampledValue from a MetricResult that carries a non-nothing variance, so arithmetic on an already-built MetricResult (e.g. inside a formula referencing a custom metric) keeps propagating variance. Returns the plain value when no variance is attached.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.newLocalScope-Tuple{String, Expr}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.newLocalScope","text":"Creates a new level in the dictionary and updates the local context with the new level\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.newLocalScopeFor-Tuple{String, Union{Expr, LineNumberNode, QuoteNode, Symbol}, Expr}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.newLocalScopeFor","text":"Creates a new level in the dictionary and updates the local context with the new level\n\nApplies to hierarchy levels with an iteration series.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.no_transform","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.no_transform","text":"Keeps the expression as it it\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.onMemoryThroughputDefinition-Tuple{Union{Expr, LineNumberNode, QuoteNode, Symbol}, PerfTest.Context, Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.onMemoryThroughputDefinition","text":"Called when a effective memory throughput macro is detected, sets up the effective memory throughput methodology\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.onRegressionDefinition-Tuple{Union{Expr, LineNumberNode, QuoteNode, Symbol}, PerfTest.Context, Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.onRegressionDefinition","text":"Called when a regression macro is detected, sets up the regression methodology. Without a threshold, the thresholds come from regression.default_threshold, see defaultRegressionThresholds.\n\nlowisbad and threshold are each either a single value, which applies to every metric, or a vector with one value per metric. A vector of another length is reported as a parse error, as is the metric :autoflop (see AUTOFLOP_UNSUPPORTED), and the macro is then ignored so the transform goes on collecting the errors of the recipe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.onRooflineDefinition-Tuple{Union{Expr, LineNumberNode, QuoteNode, Symbol}, PerfTest.Context, Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.onRooflineDefinition","text":"Called when a roofline macro is detected, sets up the roofline methodology\n\nAutomatic flop counting is not supported (see AUTOFLOPUNSUPPORTED): :autoflop in its formulas, or no actualflops while general.autoflops is true, is reported as a parse error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.openDataFile-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.openDataFile","text":"This method is used to get historical data of a performance test suite from a save file located in path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.p_blue-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.p_blue","text":"Prints the element in color blue\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.p_green-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.p_green","text":"Prints the element in color green\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.p_red-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.p_red","text":"Prints the element in color red\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.p_yellow-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.p_yellow","text":"Prints the element in color yellow\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.perftestsuffix-Tuple{PerfTest.Context}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.perftestsuffix","text":"Builds the Julia expression that goes after the performance tests, it registers, prints and saves results given the measurements done above it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.popQuoteBlocks-Tuple{Expr}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.popQuoteBlocks","text":"Useful to correct operations limited by the tree walking  Will remove quote blocks inside the main block without recursion and push  their expressions into the main block\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.printDepth!-Tuple{AbstractArray}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.printDepth!","text":"This method is used to print the test names, with consideration on the hierarchy and adding indentation whenever necessary\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.printIntervalLanding","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.printIntervalLanding","text":"This method is used to print a graphical representation on a test result and the admisible intervals it can take. The result will and the two bounds will be printed in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.printMethodology-Tuple{PerfTest.Methodology_Result, Int64, Bool}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.printMethodology","text":"This function is used to print the information relative to a methodology, relative to a a specific test execution result. This will usually print a series of metrics and might also print plots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.printMetric-Tuple{PerfTest.Metric_Result, PerfTest.Metric_Test, Int64}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.printMetric","text":"This method is used to dump into the output the information about a metric and the value obtained in a specific test.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.printfail-Tuple{BenchmarkTools.TrialJudgement, BenchmarkTools.Trial, BenchmarkTools.Trial, PerfTest.FloatRange, Int64}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.printfail","text":"This method dumps into the output a test result in case of failure. The output will be formatted to make it easy to read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.readEnergies-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.readEnergies","text":"The energy counters of devices in J, read one after the other with read_joule. Used by the GPU extensions around each execution, through a single compiled function so the reads of a sample are close together.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.regression-Tuple{Symbol, Float64, Bool, Bool}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.regression","text":"Executes regression for one single metric\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.removeBlock-Tuple{Expr}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.removeBlock","text":"Pops expr which has a head that is :block or :quote and returns array of nested expressions which are the arguments of such head.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.reportAutoflopUse!-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString, Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.reportAutoflopUse!","text":"Report as a parse error that construct (e.g. \"The @definemetric name = \"m\"\"), of the recipe being transformed, uses automatic flop counting how (e.g. \"uses :autoflop in its formula\"), which is not supported, see AUTOFLOPUNSUPPORTED. instead says what to do instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.reportRegressionLengthMismatch-Tuple{Symbol, Type, AbstractVector, AbstractVector, PerfTest.Context}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.reportRegressionLengthMismatch","text":"Report as a parse error that key (:lowisbad or :threshold) of a @regression is a vector that does not give one value per metric, see onRegressionDefinition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.resolveLiteral-Tuple{QuoteNode}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.resolveLiteral","text":"Resolves simple literal AST forms into real Julia values: a quoted symbol   (QuoteNode) becomes the symbol/value it quotes, and a vector literal   (Expr(:vect, ...)) becomes a real Vector of its (recursively resolved)   elements. Any other expression (e.g. a function call or formula) is   returned unchanged, since those parameters are meant to keep the raw AST.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.retrievePerfTests-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.retrievePerfTests","text":"retrievePerfTests(datafile_path; get=:tests, where=(x)->true, execution=:latest)\n\nGeneral-purpose retrieval over a performance-test datafile.\n\nArguments\n\ndatafile_path::AbstractString: path to a serialized Perftest_Datafile_Root.\nget::Symbol: what to return. One of:\n:tests          → Vector{Test_Result}\n:methodologies  → Vector{Methodology_Result}\n:testsets       → Vector{String} (fully-qualified testset paths)\nwhere::Function: predicate applied to each candidate element; only elements for which it returns true are kept.\nexecution::Union{Symbol, Int}: which execution to consider.\npathonly ::Bool : if true, only return the testset path for each result, instead of the full Test_Result or Methodology_Result.\n\nExamples\n\ntests  = retrievePerfTests(\"results.dat\")\nfast   = retrievePerfTests(\"results.dat\"; where = t -> hasMetric(t, :median_time))\npassed = retrievePerfTests(\"results.dat\"; where = testPassed)\nfailed = retrievePerfTests(\"results.dat\"; where = testFailed)\nmeths  = retrievePerfTests(\"results.dat\"; get = :methodologies) \n\n# All predicates:\n- `inTestSet(test, \"MySuite/MyTest\")` → true if `test` is under a testset named \"MySuite/MyTest\".\n- `hasMetric(test, :median_time)` → true if `test` has a metric\n- `hasAuxiliar(test, :memory_usage)` → true if `test` has an auxiliar metric\n- `hasPrimitive(test, :min_time)` → true if `test` has a primitive\n- `testNamed(test, \"MyTest\")` → true if `test.name == \"MyTest\"`\n- `testPassed(test)` → true if all methodologies of `test` passed\n- `testFailed(test)` → true if any methodology of `test` failed\n- `methodologyNamed(m, \"MyMethodology\")` → true if `m.name == \"MyMethodology\"`\n- `methodologyHasMetric(m, \"median_time\")` → true if `m` has a metric named \"median_time\"\n- `methodologyPassed(m)` → true if all metrics of `m` succeeded\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.rewrapDoBlockBodies-Tuple{Expr}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.rewrapDoBlockBodies","text":"MacroTools.prettify (used by treeRun to clean up the generated suite) unwraps any   block that reduces to a single statement via its flatten pass. That's unsafe for a   do-block (or any bare -> lambda) body: if the body collapses to a single non-:block   statement (e.g. a tuple literal (A, B)), flatten strips the wrapping :block, leaving   that statement sitting bare where a block is expected. Julia's own Base.show for :do   expressions doesn't guard against this (unlike its :for/:while/:function/:if/:let   siblings, which do re-wrap non-block bodies before printing) and prints the bare   expression's .args one per line, silently dropping the tuple's parens/comma and turning   \"return one value\" into \"several statements, keep only the last\".\n\nThis walks expr and re-wraps any :do-block lambda body that isn't already Expr(:block,   ...) back into one, undoing that corruption before the expression is written out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.ruleSet-Tuple{PerfTest.Context, Vector{PerfTest.ASTRule}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.ruleSet","text":"This method builds what is known as a rule set. Which is a function that will evaluate if an expression triggers a rule in a set and if that is the case apply the rule modifier. See the ASTRule documentation for more information.\n\nWARNING: the rule set will apply the FIRST rule that matches with the expression, therefore other matches will be ignored\n\nArguments\n\ncontext the context structure of the tree run, it will be ocassinally used by some rules on the set.\nrules the collection of rules that will belong to the resulting set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.runperftests-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.runperftests","text":"runperftests(file; ...)\n\n# Description\n    Simplified function to access the perftest transformation and subsequent execution of performance test suites.\n    It takes a recipe script, transforms it into a performance testing suite and executes it. The resulting suite is also saved in a file for later usage.           \n\n# Arguments\n    `file    ::AbstractString`: the path of the recipe script to be transformed\n\n# Keyword arguments\n    `execute::Bool = true`             : whether the resulting suite should be executed right after generation, by default true. If false, the resulting suite will only be saved in a file with the name of the input with and added \"_perfsuite.jl\" suffix.\n    `verbose::Union{Int,Nothing} = nothing` : level of verbosity, from 0 to 3 higher is more verbose. When given it overrides general.verbose of the config argument and of the configuration file, otherwise their value is used.\n        0 : minimal output, only warnings and errors\n        1 : general information about the transformation and execution process\n        2 : detailed information about the transformation and execution process, logs are saved in a folder\n        3 : debug level, very detailed information about the transformation and execution process\n    `clean  ::Bool = false`            : whether to leave the config file, the output test suite and the test results or delete everything after the suite has been executed, !!! including previously done results and logs CAREFUL !!!.\n    `config  ::Union{Dict{String,Any},Nothing} = nothing` : other configuration parameters to override the configuration file. Configuration priority: config macro > verbose argument > this argument > configuration file. See configuration for more info.\n    `interpreter_flags::AbstractString = \"\"` : flags of the julia process that executes the suite, split at whitespace and passed in order (e.g. \"-O3 -L setup.jl\"), --check-bounds=no and --project=. unless they set them. They cannot set the number of threads (-t, --threads), which comes from the thread setting of the suite (general.numas and general.threads_per_numa, or a @perftest_threads), it is an error.\n    `suite_flags::AbstractString = \"\"` : passed to the suite as a single argument, nothing if empty.\n\n\n# (!) Do not mistake this method for the perftest macro, which is used to set test targets inside the recipe script.\n\n# Example of a config parameter value:\n    `{\"regression\" : {\"enabled\" : true}, \"general\" : {\"recursive\" : false}}`\n\n\nSee the macro reference for more details about the recipe script format and the possible configurations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.saveDataFile-Tuple{AbstractString, PerfTest.Perftest_Datafile_Root}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.saveDataFile","text":"This method is used to save historical data of a performance test suite to a save file located in path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.saveExprAsFile","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.saveExprAsFile","text":"Utility to save an expression (expr) to a Julia file stored at path\n\nRequires a :toplevel symbol to be the head of the expression.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.saveLogFolder","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.saveLogFolder","text":"Moves the temporal log directory to the persistent directory where results are saved   and dumps all IOStream buffers to their respective log files.\n\nNothing is written, neither the folder nor the files, when general.logs_enabled is false in configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.saveMethodologyData-Tuple{AbstractString, PerfTest.Methodology_Result}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.saveMethodologyData","text":"TODO\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.setLogFolder-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.setLogFolder","text":"Creates temporal a directory for the logs of a specific execution\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.testsetUpdate!-Tuple{PerfTest.Context, String}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.testsetUpdate!","text":"Function used to register a new test set in the hierarchy record of the context, where name is the name of the test set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.toggleMPI-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.toggleMPI","text":"[!] DEPRECATED, do not use, use the Perftest configuration attributes instead. \n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.transformWith","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.transformWith","text":"Transform the recipe at path, see treeRun. source names where config comes from in the reported errors, overrides take priority over it (e.g. the verbose argument of runperftests, overrides_source names them).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.treeRun-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.treeRun","text":"This method implements the transformation that converts a recipe script into a fully-fledged testing suite. The function will return a Julia expression with the resulting performance testing suite. This can be then executed or saved in a file for later usage.\n\nArguments\n\npath the path of the script to be transformed.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nconfig configuration parameters that override the configuration file, see runperftests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.typeMatches-Tuple{Type, Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.typeMatches","text":"Like value isa declared_type, but additionally matches a Vector{ET}   union member element-wise: value is accepted if it's an AbstractVector   and every element isa ET. This covers cases Julia's own isa cannot,   because Vector is invariant in its element type (e.g. Vector{Symbol}   and Vector{Any} are not subtypes of Vector{Union{Symbol,String}} even   when every element individually satisfies Union{Symbol,String}).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.unblockAndConcat-Tuple{Vector{Expr}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.unblockAndConcat","text":"This function is useful to move expressions to the toplevel when they are enclosed inside a block\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.unionMembers-Tuple{Type}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.unionMembers","text":"Flattens a (possibly nested) Union type into a tuple of its member types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.validASTRule-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.validASTRule","text":"Constructs an ASTRule that assumes that the expression is automatically valid if matched.   Thus no validation is done.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.validateConfigurationMacro!","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.validateConfigurationMacro!","text":"Check config, parsed from a @perftestconfig string, against the configuration schema. Each entry that is not in it (along with the entries its table can have), that has an incorrect type, or that is used before the recipe is read (see PRERECIPE_KEYS) is reported as a parse error and removed, so the rest still applies to the transform. The keys whose value is interpolated are removed as well, they take their value when the suite is executed. Interpolation in a key or a table name is reported as a parse error, in which case it returns false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.validateThreadSetting!-Tuple{PerfTest.Context}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.validateThreadSetting!","text":"Check the thread setting of the suite being generated (see THREADSETTING), whether or not a @perftestthreads takes priority over it. An invalid one (see Topology.arrangementFromConfig) is reported naming where each key comes from: as a parse error when a recipe sets one of them, otherwise, as the other errors of the configuration file and of the config argument, as an error that does not stop the transform. The launcher reports it again, as it cannot launch the suite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.validateThreadsArgument-Tuple{Any, Function}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.validateThreadsArgument","text":"Check x, the argument of a @perftest_threads, each reason it is invalid is reported with report. Returns whether it is valid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.verboseOutput-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.verboseOutput","text":"Binds a channel to the standard output\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@auxiliary_metric-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@auxiliary_metric","text":"Defines a custom metric for informational purposes that will not be used for testing but will be printed as output. The metric cannot be used by other macros.\n\nArguments\n\nname : the name of the metric for identification purposes.\nunits : the unit space that the metric values will be in.\nsymbol : (optional) the symbol that identifies the metric, a Symbol or a String. Defaults to the name as a symbol (name=\"example\" -> :example).\nformula block : an expression that returns a single value, which would be the metric value. The formula can have any julia expression inside and additionally some special symbols are supported. The formula may be evaluated several times, so its applied to every target in every test set or just once, if the formula is defined inside a test set, which makes it only applicable to it.\n\nSpecial symbols:\n\n:median_time : will be substituted by the median time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:minimum_time: will be substituted by the minimum time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:ret_value : will be substituted by the return value of the target.\n:autoflop: not supported, automatic flop counting is outdated (it is always 0), a formula that uses it is reported as a parse error. Write the flop count of the target explicitly instead.\n:printed_output : will be substituted by the standard output stream of the target.\n:iterator : will be substituted by the current iterator value in a loop test set.\nAny symbol representing a custom metric or benchmark, its symbol if given, otherwise its name as a symbol (e.g metric with symbol=:ex -> :ex, metric with name=\"example\" and no symbol -> :example)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@define_benchmark-Tuple","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@define_benchmark","text":"Defines a customized reference to compare against. Must be always defined inside a testset.\n\nArguments\n\nname : (string) an identifier for the custom benchmark, methodologies that use it will refer to it using this name as a symbol, unless symbol is given.\nunits: (string) units of the reference value.\nsymbol: (optional, symbol or string) the symbol formulas and methodologies (e.g custom_benchmark=...) use to refer to the benchmark. Defaults to the name as a symbol.\nformula: an expression to calculate the reference value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@define_eff_memory_throughput-Tuple","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@define_eff_memory_throughput","text":"@define_eff_memory_throughput [kwargs...] begin {formula block} end\n\n@def_mem_thr [kwargs...] begin {formula block} end\n\nThis macro is used to define the memory bandwidth of a target in order to execute the effective memory thorughput methodology.\n\nArguments\n\nformula block : an expression that returns a single value, which would be the metric value. The formula can have any julia expression inside and additionally special symbols listed below are supported. The formula may be evaluated several times, so its applied to every target in every test set or just once, if the formula is defined inside a test set, which makes it only applicable on it and onto children testset.\nratio : the allowed minimum percentage over the maximum attainable that is allowed to pass the test, it can be a number or a Julia expression that evaluates to a number\nbenchmark : which bandwidth kernel benchmark to use (e.g :MEMSTREAMCOPY for transfer operation, :MEMBENCHSTRIAD (Shoenauer triad) for computing operations), :MEMBENCHSDAXPY (Shoenauer Daxpy) useful when not sure if copy on write is happening)\ncustom_benchmark : if a previously defined benchmark name is passed, the methodology will use it as the maximum bandwidth reference.\n\nSpecial symbols:\n\n:median_time : will be substituted by the median time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:minimum_time: will be substituted by the minimum time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:ret_value : will be substituted by the return value of the target.\n:autoflop: not supported, automatic flop counting is outdated (it is always 0), a formula that uses it is reported as a parse error. Write the flop count of the target explicitly instead.\n:printed_output : will be substituted by the standard output stream of the target.\n:iterator : will be substituted by the current iterator value in a loop test set.\nAny symbol representing a custom metric or benchmark, its symbol if given, otherwise its name as a symbol (e.g metric with symbol=:ex -> :ex, metric with name=\"example\" and no symbol -> :example)\n\nExample:\n\nThe following definition assumes that each execution of the target expression involves transacting 1000 bytes. Therefore the bandwith is 1000 / execution time.\n\n@define_eff_memory_throughput begin\n      1000 / :median_time\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@define_metric-Tuple","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@define_metric","text":"This macro is used to define a new custom metric. The metric result can be used subsequently by other macros.\n\nArguments\n\nname : the name of the metric for identification purposes.\nunits : the unit space that the metric values will be in.\nsymbol : (optional) the symbol other formulas and macros (e.g @regression metrics=...) use to refer to the metric, a Symbol or a String. Defaults to the name as a symbol (name=\"example\" -> :example).\nformula block : an expression that returns a single value, which would be the metric value. The formula can have any julia expression inside and additionally some special symbols are supported. The formula may be evaluated several times, so its applied to every target in every test set or just once, if the formula is defined inside a test set, which makes it only applicable to it. NOTE: If there is the need of referring to a variable on a formula block, it first needs to be exported using the macro @export_vars, otherwise an error will occur.\n\nSpecial symbols:\n\n:median_time : will be substituted by the median time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:minimum_time: will be substituted by the minimum time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:ret_value : will be substituted by the return value of the target.\n:autoflop: not supported, automatic flop counting is outdated (it is always 0), a formula that uses it is reported as a parse error. Write the flop count of the target explicitly instead.\n:printed_output : will be substituted by the standard output stream of the target.\n:iterator : will be substituted by the current iterator value in a loop test set.\nAny symbol representing a custom metric or benchmark, its symbol if given, otherwise its name as a symbol (e.g metric with symbol=:ex -> :ex, metric with name=\"example\" and no symbol -> :example)\n\nExample:\n\n@define_metric name=\"Effective bandwidth\" units=\"GB/s\" symbol=:bw begin\n    1e-9 * 1000 / :median_time\nend\n@define_metric name=\"Bandwidth per core\" units=\"GB/s\" begin\n    :bw / 8\nend\n@regression metrics=:bw\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@export_vars-Tuple","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@export_vars","text":"@export_vars vars...\n\nExports the specified symbols –along with the values they hold at the moment of the calling– to the scope of metric definitions. In order to use any variable on the definition of a metric such variable needs to be exported with this macro.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@lpad-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@lpad","text":"Macro that adds a space at the beggining of a string\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@on_perftest_exec-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@on_perftest_exec","text":"The expression given to this macro will only be executed in the generated suite, and will be deleted if the source code is executed as is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@on_perftest_ignore-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@on_perftest_ignore","text":"The expression given to this macro will only be executed in the source code, and will be deleted in the generated performance test suite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@perfcmp-Tuple","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@perfcmp","text":"Alias of @perfcompare\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@perfcompare-Tuple","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@perfcompare","text":"This macro is used to manually declare performance test conditions.\n\nArguments\n\nAn expression that must result to a boolean when evaluated. Being true if the comparison leads to a succesful performance test. Special symbols can be used.\n\nSpecial symbols:\n\n:median_time : will be substituted by the median time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:minimum_time: will be substituted by the minimum time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:ret_value : will be substituted by the return value of the target.\n:autoflop: not supported, automatic flop counting is outdated (it is always 0), a formula that uses it is reported as a parse error. Write the flop count of the target explicitly instead.\n:printed_output : will be substituted by the standard output stream of the target.\n:iterator : will be substituted by the current iterator value in a loop test set.\nAny symbol representing a custom metric or benchmark (e.g metric with name=\"example\" -> :example)\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nshowvar: when true, print a \"(± X)\" standard-error-of-the-mean interval alongside any auxiliary metrics in scope. Does not affect the printed TRUE/FALSE assertion result itself. Ignored (no interval shown) whenever the underlying sample count is below 30.\n\nExample:\n\n    @perfcompare :median_time < 0.05\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@perftest-Tuple","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@perftest","text":"This macro is used to signal that the wrapped expression is a performance test target, and therefore its performance will be sampled and then evaluated following the current suite configuration.\n\nIf the macro is evaluated it does not modify the target at all. The effects of the macro only show when the script is transformed into a performance testing suite.\n\nThis macro is sensitive to context since other adjacent macros can change how the target will be evaluated.\n\n@perftest [parameters...] expression\n\nRun a performance test on a given target expression.\n\nBasic usage\n\nThe simplest usage is to place @perftest in front of the expression you want to test:\n\njulia> @perftest sin(1)\n\nAdditional parameters\n\nYou can pass the following keyword arguments to configure the execution process:\n\nsetup: An expression that is run once per sample before the benchmarked expression. The setup expression is run once per sample, and is not included in the timing results. Note that each sample can require multiple evaluations.\nteardown: An expression that is run once per sample after the benchmarked expression.\nsamples: The number of samples to take. Execution will end if this many samples have been collected. Defaults to 10000.\nseconds: The number of seconds budgeted for the benchmarking process. The trial will terminate if this time is exceeded (regardless of samples), but at least one sample will always be taken. In practice, actual runtime can overshoot the budget by the duration of a sample.\nevals: The number of evaluations per sample. For best results, this should be kept consistent between trials. A good guess for this value can be automatically set on a benchmark via tune!, but using tune! can be less consistent than setting evals manually (which bypasses tuning).\ngctrial: If true, run gc() before executing this benchmark's trial. Defaults to true.\ngcsample: If true, run gc() before each sample. Defaults to false.\ntime_tolerance: The noise tolerance for the benchmark's time estimate, as a percentage. This is utilized after benchmark execution, when analyzing results. Defaults to 0.05.\n\nExamples\n\nBasic performance test\n\n @perftest sin(1)\n\nWith setup and teardown\n\nKeyword parameters must come before the target expression, which is always the last argument:\n\n @perftest setup=(data=rand(100)) teardown=(data=nothing) sort!(data)\n\nWith custom parameters\n\n# Run with a 3-second time budget\n @perftest setup=(x=rand()) seconds=3 sin(x)\n\n# Limit to 100 samples with 10 evaluations each\n @perftest samples=100 evals=10 myfunction(data)\n\n# Disable garbage collection before each sample\n @perftest gcsample=false gctrial=false allocating_function()\n\nSee Also\n\nBenchmarkTools.jl Documentation for more details on the underlying @benchmark macro and its parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@perftest_config-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@perftest_config","text":"Captures a set of configuration parameters that will override the default configuration. The parameters shall be written in TOML syntax, like a subset of the complete configuration (see config.toml generated by executing transform, or transform/configuration.jl for more information). Order is irrelevant. This macro shall be put as high as possible in the test file (code that is above will be transformed using the default configuration).\n\nRecursive transformation:\n\nThis macro will set the new configuration keys for the current file and any other included files. If the included files have the macro as well, those macros will override the configuration locally for each file.\n\nValidation:\n\nThe keys are checked when the recipe is transformed. A section or key that does not exist (the error lists the existing ones), a key with an incorrect type, a key that is used before the recipe is read (MPI.enabled, set it in the configuration file or with the config argument of runperftests or PerfTest.transform instead) and invalid TOML are reported as parse errors, so no suite is generated. The valid keys of the macro still apply to the rest of the transform, so its other errors are reported as well.\n\nInterpolation:\n\nValues can be interpolated inside TOML strings, e.g. dedicated_reference_file = 'ref_$(gethostname()).JLD2'. Those keys take their value when the generated suite is executed. An interpolated value is always a string, so only keys that take a String can be interpolated.\n\nArguments\n\nA string literal, with the TOML declaration of configuration keys. It is read when the recipe is transformed, so a variable or any other expression is reported as a parse error.\n\nExample\n\n@perftestconfig \" [roofline]   enabled = false [general]   maxsaved_results = 1   recursive = false \"\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@perftest_threads-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@perftest_threads","text":"This macro can be used to specify one or more thread pinning configurations for the performance test suite. Not calling this macro on the recipe leaves the threads unpinned.\n\n# Arguments\n    A thread specification or a list of them. A thread specification can be:\n    (#numas, #threads_per_numa) or a shortcut symbol like :single, :numa or :all\n    Example:\n        (1, 1) or :single   -> One thread on one numa node\n        (1, 2)              -> Two threads on two numa nodes\n        (1, 1//1) or :numa  -> All threads on one numa node\n        (1, 1//2)           -> Half of the threads available for one numa node\n        (2, 1)              -> One thread on each of two numa nodes \n        (1//1,1//1) or :all -> All posible threads on all numa nodes\n\n    Alternatively a list of integers can be passed which will be interpreted as a manual pinning.\n\n    The argument must be a literal, as it is read when the recipe is transformed: a variable or any other expression,\n    as well as an invalid arrangement, is reported as a parse error.\n\n    The arrangement is resolved on the machine that executes the suite, e.g. :numa takes the size of a NUMA domain of that machine.\n\n    It takes priority over the thread setting of the configuration (general.numas and general.threads_per_numa) for\n    the number of threads runperftests launches the suite with.\n\n    [!] As of now, the pinning is not MPI aware, (this will come in PerfTest v1.2.4) in MPI cases use the manual pinning, with the list of integers.\n    [!] This is a feature in development, more comprehensive features will be built over this later on (1.2.4).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@regression-Tuple","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@regression","text":"This macro is used to define regression tests with a customized configuration. Redefining the macro in the same testset will overwrite the previous configuration.\n\nA test compares the ratio new/reference of each metric, between the new value and the one of the reference execution, with its threshold.\n\nArguments\n\nlowisbad: whether a lower value of the metric is bad, true by default. With low_is_bad=true (e.g. for a throughput) the test passes if the ratio is above the threshold, with low_is_bad=false (e.g. for a time) if it is below. It can be a single boolean that applies to all metrics or a vector of booleans that applies to each metric separately.\nthreshold : the ratio new/reference the metric must stay above (low_is_bad=true, e.g. 0.9 fails if the new value is 90% of the reference or less) or below (low_is_bad=false, e.g. 1.1 fails if the new value is 110% of the reference or more). It can be a single float that applies to all metrics or a vector of floats that applies to each metric separately. Without it, each metric takes its threshold from the configuration key regression.default_threshold (1.1 by default): the value itself for a metric with low_is_bad=false, and 2 − that value for one with low_is_bad=true (1.1 gives 0.9), so the default tolerates a 10% change in the bad direction either way. A regression.default_threshold of 2 or more leaves no threshold for a metric with low_is_bad=true, it is reported as a parse error.\nmetrics: track regression in the metrics whose names are passed as argument, it accepts a single string or a vector of strings. Non-existent metrics are ignored.\nshowvar: when true, print a \"(± X)\" standard-error-of-the-mean interval alongside the ratio metric and any auxiliary metrics in scope. Ignored (no interval shown) whenever the underlying sample count is below 30.\n\nExamples:\n\n    # Tests will fail if the median time is 110% of the reference value or more\n    @regression threshold=1.1 low_is_bad=false metrics=:median_time\n\n    # Tests will fail if the metric results are 90% of the reference value or less\n    @regression threshold=0.9 low_is_bad=true metrics=[:custom_metric1, :custom_metric2]\n\n    # Without threshold, with the default regression.default_threshold = 1.1: the median time fails at 110% of the\n    # reference or more (threshold 1.1), custom_metric1 at 90% or less (threshold 2 − 1.1 = 0.9)\n    @regression low_is_bad=[false, true] metrics=[:median_time, :custom_metric1]\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.@roofline","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.@roofline","text":"This macro enables roofline modelling, if put just before a target declaration (@perftest) it will proceed to evaluate it using a roofline model.\n\nMandatory arguments\n\nformula block: the macro has to wrap a block that holds a formula to obtain the operational intensity of target algorithms.\n\nOptional arguments\n\ncpu_peak : a manual input value for the maximum attainable FLOPS, this will override the empirical runtime benchmark\nmembw_peak : a manual input value for the maximum memory bandwith, this will override the empirical runtime benchmark\ntarget_opint : a desired operational intensity for the target, this will turn operational intensity into a test metric\nactual_flops: the flop count of the target, a number or a formula (as the operational intensity one), e.g. actual_flops = 2 * N. The attained flops of the test are actual_flops / :median_time. Give it to test the attained flops: automatic flop counting is outdated and not supported, so a @roofline without actual_flops while the configuration key general.autoflops is true is reported as a parse error.\ntarget_ratio : the acceptable ratio between the actual performance and the projected performance from the roofline, this will turn actual performance into a test metric.\nmem_benchmark: which benchmark to use for the roofline, the default is the Shoenauer DAXPY (:MEMBENCHDAXPY), custom benchmarks are supported. Other benchmarks: [:MEMSTREAMCOPY,:MEMSTREAMADD, :MEMBENCHSTRIAD, :MEMBENCHSDAXPY]\n\nSpecial symbols:\n\n:median_time : will be substituted by the median time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:minimum_time: will be substituted by the minimum time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:ret_value : will be substituted by the return value of the target.\n:autoflop: not supported, automatic flop counting is outdated (it is always 0), a formula that uses it, actual_flops = :autoflop included, is reported as a parse error. Give the flop count with actual_flops instead.\n:printed_output : will be substituted by the standard output stream of the target.\n:iterator : will be substituted by the current iterator value in a loop test set.\n\nAny formula block specified in this macro supports these symbols.\n\nExample\n\n    # Inner product of two vectors of N Float64 values: 2N flops over 16N bytes\n    @roofline actual_flops = 2 * N target_ratio = 0.05 begin\n        (2 * N) / (16 * N)\n    end\n\nThe code block defines operational intensity, whilst the other arguments define how to measure and compare the actual performance with the roofline performance. If the actual to projected performance ratio goes below the target, the test fails.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.describe_unknown-Tuple{Any, Any, AbstractString, Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.describe_unknown","text":"Describe an entry of a configuration that is not in the schema, e.g. a misspelled key.\n\nArgs:\n\nkey: Name of the entry in its table\nvalue: Value of the entry\nprefix: Full name of its table followed by a dot, empty at the top level\nschema: Schema of its table\n\nReturns:\n\nIts kind and full name, e.g. \"key general.verbse\" or \"section genral\", and the entries its table can have, e.g. \"The keys of section general are: autoflops, ...\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.fill_config","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.fill_config","text":"Complete a possibly partial configuration following a schema.\n\nArgs:\n\nconfig: Dictionary to complete, e.g. the contents of a configuration file\nbase: Configuration that provides the missing entries\nschema: Dictionary defining expected structure and types\nsource: Where config comes from (e.g. \"the configuration file perftest_config.toml\"), named in the reported errors\n\nReturns:\n\nA new configuration dictionary that shares no data with config or base. Entries missing from config or with an incorrect type (reported as an error) take their value from base, entries outside the schema are kept.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.load_bencher_config","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.load_bencher_config","text":"Set the [bencher] section of the current configuration from the configuration file, used by the generated suites when they start executing so the Bencher credentials (e.g. apikey) are never written into them. Unlike `loadconfig`, a missing file is not created, the default values are used instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.load_config","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.load_config","text":"Load configuration from a TOML file over the default configuration, and make it the current one.\n\nArgs:\n\nfilepath: Path to the TOML file, CONFIG_FILE in the working directory if empty\n\nReturns:\n\nLoaded configuration dictionary. If the file does not exist it is created with the default configuration, if it cannot be parsed it is left untouched and the default configuration is used. Its entries that are not in the schema are reported as errors and ignored, the rest of the file still applies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.load_config-Tuple{Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.load_config","text":"Make a copy of config the current configuration, missing or malformed entries take their default value. source is where config comes from, named in the reported errors and recorded in SOURCES for the entries that apply.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.merge_configs","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.merge_configs","text":"Recursively merge two configs, with values from override_config taking precedence.\n\nEach entry of overrideconfig is checked against the schema on its own: one with an incorrect type is reported as an error, naming source (where overrideconfig comes from) if given, and ignored, the rest still apply. Entries outside the schema are kept as they are.\n\nReturns:\n\nA new configuration dictionary that shares no data with base_config or override_config\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.pop_config-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.pop_config","text":"Restore the configuration saved by the last push_config.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.push_config-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.push_config","text":"Save a copy of the current configuration, so it can be changed (e.g. by an included recipe) and later restored by pop_config.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.record_sources!","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.record_sources!","text":"Record source in sources (see SOURCES) as where the entries of config come from, the ones that apply when it is loaded or merged: those in the schema with the expected type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.report_unknown_entries","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.report_unknown_entries","text":"Report as errors the entries of a configuration read from source (e.g. \"the configuration file perftest_config.toml\") that are not in the schema. They are ignored, the rest of the configuration still applies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.save_config","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.save_config","text":"Save configuration to a TOML file.\n\nArgs:\n\nconfig: Configuration dictionary\nfilepath: Path to save the TOML file\nschema: Optional schema for validation\n\nReturns:\n\nBoolean indicating successful save\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.source_of-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.source_of","text":"Where the value in force of a configuration key comes from (e.g. \"the configuration file perftestconfig.toml\"), by its full name (e.g. \"regression.defaultthreshold\"), see SOURCES.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.unknown_entries","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.unknown_entries","text":"Find the entries of a possibly partial configuration that are not in a schema, described as in describe_unknown. The contents of an unknown section are not looked into.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.Configuration.validate_config-Tuple{Dict, Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.Configuration.validate_config","text":"Validate configuration against a predefined schema.\n\nArgs:\n\nconfig: Dictionary to validate\nschema: Dictionary defining expected structure and types\n\nReturns:\n\nBoolean indicating whether configuration is valid\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.add_body_field!-Tuple{PerfTest.BencherREST.RESTRequestBuilder, String, Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.add_body_field!","text":"add_body_field!(builder::RESTRequestBuilder, key::String, value)\n\nAdd a single field to the request body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.add_header!-Tuple{PerfTest.BencherREST.RESTRequestBuilder, String, String}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.add_header!","text":"add_header!(builder::RESTRequestBuilder, key::String, value::String)\n\nAdd a single header to the request.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.add_headers!-Tuple{PerfTest.BencherREST.RESTRequestBuilder, Dict{String, String}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.add_headers!","text":"add_headers!(builder::RESTRequestBuilder, headers::Dict{String, String})\n\nAdd multiple headers to the request.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.add_query_param!-Tuple{PerfTest.BencherREST.RESTRequestBuilder, String, Any}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.add_query_param!","text":"add_query_param!(builder::RESTRequestBuilder, key::String, value)\n\nAdd a single query parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.add_query_params!-Tuple{PerfTest.BencherREST.RESTRequestBuilder, Dict{String, Any}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.add_query_params!","text":"add_query_params!(builder::RESTRequestBuilder, params::Dict{String, Any})\n\nAdd multiple query parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.build-Tuple{PerfTest.BencherREST.RESTRequestBuilder}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.build","text":"build(builder::RESTRequestBuilder)\n\nBuild and return the request dictionary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.create_branch-Tuple{String, String, Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.create_branch","text":"create_branch(project_uuid::String, branch_name::String)\n\nCreate a new branch in the project.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.create_measure-Tuple{String, PerfTest.Metric_Result, Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.create_measure","text":"create_measure(project_uuid::String, metric::BencherMetric)\n\nCreate a new measure in the project.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.create_project-Tuple{String, Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.create_project","text":"create_project(project_name::String)\n\nCreate a new project in the organization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.create_testbed-Tuple{String, String, Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.create_testbed","text":"create_testbed(project_uuid::String, testbed_name::String)\n\nCreate a new testbed in the project.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.ensure_bencher_resources-Tuple{Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.ensure_bencher_resources","text":"ensure_bencher_resources()\n\nEnsure that the current project, branch, and testbed exist in Bencher. Returns a tuple of (project, branch, testbed) with their respective information.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.exportSuiteToBencher-Tuple{PerfTest.Perftest_Datafile_Root, Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.exportSuiteToBencher","text":"\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.get_all_measures-Tuple{String, Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.get_all_measures","text":"get_all_measures(project_uuid::String)\n\nGet all measures for a project.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.get_current_git_branch-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.get_current_git_branch","text":"get_current_git_branch()\n\nGet the current git branch name from the repository.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.get_testbed_name-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.get_testbed_name","text":"get_testbed_name()\n\nGet the testbed name based on hostname and architecture.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.make_request-Tuple{Dict{String, Any}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.make_request","text":"make_request(request_dict::Dict{String, Any})\n\nExecute a REST request based on the provided dictionary.\n\nReturns a tuple of (success::Bool, responsedata::Any, errormessage::String)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.prepare_benchmark_results-Tuple{Dict{String, Any}, Dict{String, Any}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.prepare_benchmark_results","text":"prepare_benchmark_results(raw_results::Dict{String, Any}, measures_dict::Dict{String, Any})\n\nPrepare benchmark results for submission to Bencher. Filters out any metrics that don't have corresponding measures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.processTestHierarchy-Tuple{Dict{String, Union{PerfTest.Test_Result, Dict}}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.processTestHierarchy","text":"TODO flatten_test_hierarchy(results::Dict{String, Union{Dict, Test_Result}}, prefix::String=\"\") -> Dict{String, Test_Result}\n\nRecursively flatten a nested dictionary of test results into a single-level dictionary. The hierarchy levels are incorporated into the keys using \"::\" as a separator.\n\nExample:     {         \"level1\": {             \"level2\": {                 \"benchmarkname\": TestResult             }         }     }\n\nbecomes:\n\n{\n    \"level1::level2::benchmark_name\": Test_Result\n}\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.quick_get-Tuple{String}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.quick_get","text":"quick_get(url::String; headers::Dict{String, String}=Dict{String, String}(), \n        query_params::Dict{String, Any}=Dict{String, Any}())\n\nConvenience function for simple GET requests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.quick_post-Tuple{String, Dict{String, Any}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.quick_post","text":"quick_post(url::String, body::Dict{String, Any}; \n        headers::Dict{String, String}=Dict{String, String}())\n\nConvenience function for simple POST requests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.rfc3339_formatter-Tuple{Dates.DateTime}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.rfc3339_formatter","text":"\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.search_branch-Tuple{String, String, Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.search_branch","text":"search_branch(project_uuid::String, branch_name::String)\n\nSearch for a branch by name in the project.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.search_measure-Tuple{String, String, Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.search_measure","text":"search_measure(project_uuid::String, measure_name::String)\n\nSearch for a measure by name in the project.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.search_project-Tuple{String, Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.search_project","text":"search_project(project_name::String)\n\nSearch for a project by name in the organization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.search_testbed-Tuple{String, String, Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.search_testbed","text":"search_testbed(project_uuid::String, testbed_name::String)\n\nSearch for a testbed by name in the project.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.set_api_key!-Tuple{PerfTest.BencherREST.RESTRequestBuilder, String}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.set_api_key!","text":"set_api_key!(builder::RESTRequestBuilder, api_key::String; header_name::String=\"X-API-Key\")\n\nConvenience method to set API key in headers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.set_bearer_token!-Tuple{PerfTest.BencherREST.RESTRequestBuilder, String}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.set_bearer_token!","text":"set_bearer_token!(builder::RESTRequestBuilder, token::String)\n\nConvenience method to set Bearer token for authentication.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.set_body!-Tuple{PerfTest.BencherREST.RESTRequestBuilder, Dict{String, Any}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.set_body!","text":"set_body!(builder::RESTRequestBuilder, body::Dict{String, Any})\n\nSet the request body (for POST, PUT requests).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.set_method!-Tuple{PerfTest.BencherREST.RESTRequestBuilder, String}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.set_method!","text":"set_method!(builder::RESTRequestBuilder, method::String)\n\nSet the HTTP method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.set_timeout!-Tuple{PerfTest.BencherREST.RESTRequestBuilder, Int64}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.set_timeout!","text":"set_timeout!(builder::RESTRequestBuilder, timeout::Int)\n\nSet the request timeout in seconds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.set_url!-Tuple{PerfTest.BencherREST.RESTRequestBuilder, String}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.set_url!","text":"set_url!(builder::RESTRequestBuilder, url::String)\n\nSet the target URL.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.submitReport-Tuple{Dict, Dict}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.submitReport","text":"\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#PerfTest.BencherREST.validate_metric_results-Tuple{Dict{String, Any}, Dict{String, Any}}","page":"API reference","title":"PerfTest.BencherREST.validate_metric_results","text":"validate_metric_results(results::Dict{String, Any}, measures_dict::Dict{String, Any})\n\nValidate that all metrics in the results have corresponding measures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"examples/mock4-recursive/#Recursive-Suite-Generation","page":"Recursive Suite Generation","title":"Recursive Suite Generation","text":"This mock example explains how PerfTest can recursively integrate files into a performance test suite.\n\nusing Test\nusing PerfTest\n\n\n# Note the config here disables verbosity but the nested files enable it, inside the nested file its config has priority\n@perftest_config \"\n[regression]\nenabled = false\n[general]\nverbose = false\n\"\n\n@testset \"A\" begin\n    @testset \"A.1\" begin\n        # Check that time elapsed is less than one second, applies to the targets inside this testset\n        @perfcompare :median_time < 1\n        # Being \"mock3-roofline.jl\" a file with the roofline mock example source code.\n        include(\"mock3-roofline.jl\")\n    end\n    include(\"mock3-roofline.jl\")\nend\n\nThis will execute the same roofline test but under different hierarchies, also the first one will have an additional performance metric assertion with perfcompare. All test are successful.","category":"section"},{"location":"usage/#Quickstart","page":"Quickstart","title":"Quickstart","text":"PerfTest provides a set of macros to instrument ordinary Julia test files with performance tests. The idea is to have the posibility of having a functional and a performance suite all in the same place.\n\nThe underlying idea of declaring performance tests can be boiled down the following:\n\nHave a @testset that groups tests for a software unit\nTell PerfTest what is the target to be tested by using the macro @perftest\nTell PerfTest how the target shall be tested, which metrics are interesting, which of those metrics values would be considered a failure, this can be declared using the metric and methodology macros (see Macros)\n\nThe following dummy example presents how a recipe file looks like:\n\nusing ExampleModule : innerProduct, Test, PerfTest   # Importing the target and test libraries\n@testset \"Roofline Test\" begin\n    a,b = rand(1e6),rand(1e6)\n\n    @roofline actual_flops=2e6 target_ratio=0.5 begin\n        2e6 / (2 * 8 * 1e6)\n    end\n\n    @perftest innerProduct(a, b)\n    @test innerProduct(a,b) == sum(a .* b)\nend\n\nThe following things can be appreciated in this example:\n\nThis is a combined functional and performance unit test suite (there is both @test and @perftest present)\nThe target of the perftest is the innerProduct function\nThe performance test methodology is a roofline model, the developer expects innerProduct to perform at least at 50% of the maximum flop performance set by the roofline. The operational intensity is defined on the main block of the macro, and actual_flops gives the flop count of the target (2 flops per element of the vectors), from which the attained flops are computed. Automatic flop counting (:autoflop) is outdated and not supported, so the flop count is always given explicitly.","category":"section"},{"location":"usage/#How-to-use,-a-first-PerfTest.jl-recipe","page":"Quickstart","title":"How to use, a first PerfTest.jl recipe","text":"Lets assume we are a developer that wants to track performance regressions on the components of a package in development. The files discussed here can be accessed in examples/example_quickstart. We have the following module:\n\n# module.jl\nmodule MyPackage\n\n# Add [a[1],a[2],...a[end]] and [b[end], b[end-1],...,b[1]] elementwise\nfunction addReversed(A :: Vector{<: Number}, B:: Vector{<: Number}) :: Vector{<:Number}\n   return [a + b for (a,b) in zip(A, reverse(B))]\nend\n\nend\n\nWe want to track the performance of this package in order to detect performance regressions. To do so we build the following test recipe:\n\n# testfile.jl\nusing Test,PerfTest\n\ninclude(\"module.jl\")\n\n@perftest_config \"\n[general]\nverbose = 3\n[regression]\ndedicated_reference_file='reference.JLD2'\n\"\n\n@testset \"addReversed tests\" begin\n    N = 10\n    # We want the size to be bigger on the performance test\n    @on_perftest_exec begin\n        N = 1_000_000\n    end\n    # We set the regression checker, we dont specify a metric therefore the default (median time elapsed) is used\n    # low_is_bad=false time elapsed metrics are considered worse the bigger they are\n    # threshold = 1.05 the test will fail if the time is 105% of the reference or greater, in other words: @test time_elapsed < 1.05 * reference\n    @regression threshold=1.05 low_is_bad=false\n\n    A = [i for i in 1:N]\n    B = [N-i for i in 1:N]\n\n    result = @perftest MyPackage.addReversed(A, B)\n    @test sum(result) == N*N \nend","category":"section"},{"location":"usage/#Running-the-functional-test-side-of-the-recipe:","page":"Quickstart","title":"Running the functional test side of the recipe:","text":"    include(\"testfile.jl\")\n\nOr if the module is setup as its own package (not this example):\n\n    using Pkg; Pkg.test()","category":"section"},{"location":"usage/#Running-the-performance-test-side-of-the-recipe:","page":"Quickstart","title":"Running the performance test side of the recipe:","text":"We are doing regression, so in case no reference has been made we will need to execute it at least twice. Once to record the reference, and after that whenever the developer wants to check for a performance regression.\n\nThe first time PerfTest is run on a specific directory, a configuration file will be created with a set of default values. Configuration parameters can be set as well by the @perftest_config macro, as seen above. The macro takes the highest priority over any other configuration source.\n\n    # This will record a successful performance suite\n    using PerfTest; runperftests(\"testfile.jl\")\n\nIn between, the developer may add new changes to the implementation or checks out a different git branch.\n\n    # This will compare the suite results against the reference, if the tests are sucessful the results become the reference.\n    using PerfTest; runperftests(\"testfile.jl\")\n\nFor more information have a look at the Examples and see the API reference for details on the usage of PerfTest.","category":"section"},{"location":"usage/#Main-use-cases-of-this-package:","page":"Quickstart","title":"Main use cases of this package:","text":"This package supports both performance testing by regression checks and by performance methodologies, e.g roofline models. It is meant to cover the following two situations:\n\n1. A developer wants to track potential regressions over the course of package development.\n2. A developer wants to set machine-agnostic performance tests by using methodologies or custom metrics that are normalized by machine parameters, with the purpose of verifying that the package profits from the capabilities of the machines its been executed on.\n    2.1. During development.\n    2.2. During the whole software lifecycle, users can check if the package is properly set up in their machine.","category":"section"},{"location":"usage/#Installation","page":"Quickstart","title":"Installation","text":"PerfTest can be installed directly with the Julia package manager from the Julia REPL:\n\n  using Pkg\n  Pkg.add(\"https://github.com/JuliaPerf/PerfTest.jl.git\")","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/#Examples","page":"...","title":"Examples","text":"Pages = [\"examples/basic.md\"]\n\nPages = [\"examples/roofline.md\"]","category":"section"},{"location":"macros/#PerfTest-macros-quick-reference","page":"Macros","title":"PerfTest macros quick reference","text":"The following are the main macros used to define performance test suites. These shall be always used inside a testset (see the [Test] package). Combining the different macros listed in this section gives the full extent of the package features.","category":"section"},{"location":"macros/#Declaring-test-targets","page":"Macros","title":"Declaring test targets","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"macros/#Declaring-metrics","page":"Macros","title":"Declaring metrics","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"macros/#Declaring-methodologies","page":"Macros","title":"Declaring methodologies","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"macros/#Structure-and-configuration","page":"Macros","title":"Structure and configuration","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"macros/#PerfTest.@perftest","page":"Macros","title":"PerfTest.@perftest","text":"This macro is used to signal that the wrapped expression is a performance test target, and therefore its performance will be sampled and then evaluated following the current suite configuration.\n\nIf the macro is evaluated it does not modify the target at all. The effects of the macro only show when the script is transformed into a performance testing suite.\n\nThis macro is sensitive to context since other adjacent macros can change how the target will be evaluated.\n\n@perftest [parameters...] expression\n\nRun a performance test on a given target expression.\n\nBasic usage\n\nThe simplest usage is to place @perftest in front of the expression you want to test:\n\njulia> @perftest sin(1)\n\nAdditional parameters\n\nYou can pass the following keyword arguments to configure the execution process:\n\nsetup: An expression that is run once per sample before the benchmarked expression. The setup expression is run once per sample, and is not included in the timing results. Note that each sample can require multiple evaluations.\nteardown: An expression that is run once per sample after the benchmarked expression.\nsamples: The number of samples to take. Execution will end if this many samples have been collected. Defaults to 10000.\nseconds: The number of seconds budgeted for the benchmarking process. The trial will terminate if this time is exceeded (regardless of samples), but at least one sample will always be taken. In practice, actual runtime can overshoot the budget by the duration of a sample.\nevals: The number of evaluations per sample. For best results, this should be kept consistent between trials. A good guess for this value can be automatically set on a benchmark via tune!, but using tune! can be less consistent than setting evals manually (which bypasses tuning).\ngctrial: If true, run gc() before executing this benchmark's trial. Defaults to true.\ngcsample: If true, run gc() before each sample. Defaults to false.\ntime_tolerance: The noise tolerance for the benchmark's time estimate, as a percentage. This is utilized after benchmark execution, when analyzing results. Defaults to 0.05.\n\nExamples\n\nBasic performance test\n\n @perftest sin(1)\n\nWith setup and teardown\n\nKeyword parameters must come before the target expression, which is always the last argument:\n\n @perftest setup=(data=rand(100)) teardown=(data=nothing) sort!(data)\n\nWith custom parameters\n\n# Run with a 3-second time budget\n @perftest setup=(x=rand()) seconds=3 sin(x)\n\n# Limit to 100 samples with 10 evaluations each\n @perftest samples=100 evals=10 myfunction(data)\n\n# Disable garbage collection before each sample\n @perftest gcsample=false gctrial=false allocating_function()\n\nSee Also\n\nBenchmarkTools.jl Documentation for more details on the underlying @benchmark macro and its parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"macros/#PerfTest.@define_metric","page":"Macros","title":"PerfTest.@define_metric","text":"This macro is used to define a new custom metric. The metric result can be used subsequently by other macros.\n\nArguments\n\nname : the name of the metric for identification purposes.\nunits : the unit space that the metric values will be in.\nsymbol : (optional) the symbol other formulas and macros (e.g @regression metrics=...) use to refer to the metric, a Symbol or a String. Defaults to the name as a symbol (name=\"example\" -> :example).\nformula block : an expression that returns a single value, which would be the metric value. The formula can have any julia expression inside and additionally some special symbols are supported. The formula may be evaluated several times, so its applied to every target in every test set or just once, if the formula is defined inside a test set, which makes it only applicable to it. NOTE: If there is the need of referring to a variable on a formula block, it first needs to be exported using the macro @export_vars, otherwise an error will occur.\n\nSpecial symbols:\n\n:median_time : will be substituted by the median time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:minimum_time: will be substituted by the minimum time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:ret_value : will be substituted by the return value of the target.\n:autoflop: not supported, automatic flop counting is outdated (it is always 0), a formula that uses it is reported as a parse error. Write the flop count of the target explicitly instead.\n:printed_output : will be substituted by the standard output stream of the target.\n:iterator : will be substituted by the current iterator value in a loop test set.\nAny symbol representing a custom metric or benchmark, its symbol if given, otherwise its name as a symbol (e.g metric with symbol=:ex -> :ex, metric with name=\"example\" and no symbol -> :example)\n\nExample:\n\n@define_metric name=\"Effective bandwidth\" units=\"GB/s\" symbol=:bw begin\n    1e-9 * 1000 / :median_time\nend\n@define_metric name=\"Bandwidth per core\" units=\"GB/s\" begin\n    :bw / 8\nend\n@regression metrics=:bw\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"macros/#PerfTest.@auxiliary_metric","page":"Macros","title":"PerfTest.@auxiliary_metric","text":"Defines a custom metric for informational purposes that will not be used for testing but will be printed as output. The metric cannot be used by other macros.\n\nArguments\n\nname : the name of the metric for identification purposes.\nunits : the unit space that the metric values will be in.\nsymbol : (optional) the symbol that identifies the metric, a Symbol or a String. Defaults to the name as a symbol (name=\"example\" -> :example).\nformula block : an expression that returns a single value, which would be the metric value. The formula can have any julia expression inside and additionally some special symbols are supported. The formula may be evaluated several times, so its applied to every target in every test set or just once, if the formula is defined inside a test set, which makes it only applicable to it.\n\nSpecial symbols:\n\n:median_time : will be substituted by the median time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:minimum_time: will be substituted by the minimum time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:ret_value : will be substituted by the return value of the target.\n:autoflop: not supported, automatic flop counting is outdated (it is always 0), a formula that uses it is reported as a parse error. Write the flop count of the target explicitly instead.\n:printed_output : will be substituted by the standard output stream of the target.\n:iterator : will be substituted by the current iterator value in a loop test set.\nAny symbol representing a custom metric or benchmark, its symbol if given, otherwise its name as a symbol (e.g metric with symbol=:ex -> :ex, metric with name=\"example\" and no symbol -> :example)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"macros/#PerfTest.@perfcompare","page":"Macros","title":"PerfTest.@perfcompare","text":"This macro is used to manually declare performance test conditions.\n\nArguments\n\nAn expression that must result to a boolean when evaluated. Being true if the comparison leads to a succesful performance test. Special symbols can be used.\n\nSpecial symbols:\n\n:median_time : will be substituted by the median time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:minimum_time: will be substituted by the minimum time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:ret_value : will be substituted by the return value of the target.\n:autoflop: not supported, automatic flop counting is outdated (it is always 0), a formula that uses it is reported as a parse error. Write the flop count of the target explicitly instead.\n:printed_output : will be substituted by the standard output stream of the target.\n:iterator : will be substituted by the current iterator value in a loop test set.\nAny symbol representing a custom metric or benchmark (e.g metric with name=\"example\" -> :example)\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nshowvar: when true, print a \"(± X)\" standard-error-of-the-mean interval alongside any auxiliary metrics in scope. Does not affect the printed TRUE/FALSE assertion result itself. Ignored (no interval shown) whenever the underlying sample count is below 30.\n\nExample:\n\n    @perfcompare :median_time < 0.05\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"macros/#PerfTest.@define_eff_memory_throughput","page":"Macros","title":"PerfTest.@define_eff_memory_throughput","text":"@define_eff_memory_throughput [kwargs...] begin {formula block} end\n\n@def_mem_thr [kwargs...] begin {formula block} end\n\nThis macro is used to define the memory bandwidth of a target in order to execute the effective memory thorughput methodology.\n\nArguments\n\nformula block : an expression that returns a single value, which would be the metric value. The formula can have any julia expression inside and additionally special symbols listed below are supported. The formula may be evaluated several times, so its applied to every target in every test set or just once, if the formula is defined inside a test set, which makes it only applicable on it and onto children testset.\nratio : the allowed minimum percentage over the maximum attainable that is allowed to pass the test, it can be a number or a Julia expression that evaluates to a number\nbenchmark : which bandwidth kernel benchmark to use (e.g :MEMSTREAMCOPY for transfer operation, :MEMBENCHSTRIAD (Shoenauer triad) for computing operations), :MEMBENCHSDAXPY (Shoenauer Daxpy) useful when not sure if copy on write is happening)\ncustom_benchmark : if a previously defined benchmark name is passed, the methodology will use it as the maximum bandwidth reference.\n\nSpecial symbols:\n\n:median_time : will be substituted by the median time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:minimum_time: will be substituted by the minimum time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:ret_value : will be substituted by the return value of the target.\n:autoflop: not supported, automatic flop counting is outdated (it is always 0), a formula that uses it is reported as a parse error. Write the flop count of the target explicitly instead.\n:printed_output : will be substituted by the standard output stream of the target.\n:iterator : will be substituted by the current iterator value in a loop test set.\nAny symbol representing a custom metric or benchmark, its symbol if given, otherwise its name as a symbol (e.g metric with symbol=:ex -> :ex, metric with name=\"example\" and no symbol -> :example)\n\nExample:\n\nThe following definition assumes that each execution of the target expression involves transacting 1000 bytes. Therefore the bandwith is 1000 / execution time.\n\n@define_eff_memory_throughput begin\n      1000 / :median_time\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"macros/#PerfTest.@roofline","page":"Macros","title":"PerfTest.@roofline","text":"This macro enables roofline modelling, if put just before a target declaration (@perftest) it will proceed to evaluate it using a roofline model.\n\nMandatory arguments\n\nformula block: the macro has to wrap a block that holds a formula to obtain the operational intensity of target algorithms.\n\nOptional arguments\n\ncpu_peak : a manual input value for the maximum attainable FLOPS, this will override the empirical runtime benchmark\nmembw_peak : a manual input value for the maximum memory bandwith, this will override the empirical runtime benchmark\ntarget_opint : a desired operational intensity for the target, this will turn operational intensity into a test metric\nactual_flops: the flop count of the target, a number or a formula (as the operational intensity one), e.g. actual_flops = 2 * N. The attained flops of the test are actual_flops / :median_time. Give it to test the attained flops: automatic flop counting is outdated and not supported, so a @roofline without actual_flops while the configuration key general.autoflops is true is reported as a parse error.\ntarget_ratio : the acceptable ratio between the actual performance and the projected performance from the roofline, this will turn actual performance into a test metric.\nmem_benchmark: which benchmark to use for the roofline, the default is the Shoenauer DAXPY (:MEMBENCHDAXPY), custom benchmarks are supported. Other benchmarks: [:MEMSTREAMCOPY,:MEMSTREAMADD, :MEMBENCHSTRIAD, :MEMBENCHSDAXPY]\n\nSpecial symbols:\n\n:median_time : will be substituted by the median time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:minimum_time: will be substituted by the minimum time the target took to execute in the benchmark.\n:ret_value : will be substituted by the return value of the target.\n:autoflop: not supported, automatic flop counting is outdated (it is always 0), a formula that uses it, actual_flops = :autoflop included, is reported as a parse error. Give the flop count with actual_flops instead.\n:printed_output : will be substituted by the standard output stream of the target.\n:iterator : will be substituted by the current iterator value in a loop test set.\n\nAny formula block specified in this macro supports these symbols.\n\nExample\n\n    # Inner product of two vectors of N Float64 values: 2N flops over 16N bytes\n    @roofline actual_flops = 2 * N target_ratio = 0.05 begin\n        (2 * N) / (16 * N)\n    end\n\nThe code block defines operational intensity, whilst the other arguments define how to measure and compare the actual performance with the roofline performance. If the actual to projected performance ratio goes below the target, the test fails.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"macros/#PerfTest.@perftest_config","page":"Macros","title":"PerfTest.@perftest_config","text":"Captures a set of configuration parameters that will override the default configuration. The parameters shall be written in TOML syntax, like a subset of the complete configuration (see config.toml generated by executing transform, or transform/configuration.jl for more information). Order is irrelevant. This macro shall be put as high as possible in the test file (code that is above will be transformed using the default configuration).\n\nRecursive transformation:\n\nThis macro will set the new configuration keys for the current file and any other included files. If the included files have the macro as well, those macros will override the configuration locally for each file.\n\nValidation:\n\nThe keys are checked when the recipe is transformed. A section or key that does not exist (the error lists the existing ones), a key with an incorrect type, a key that is used before the recipe is read (MPI.enabled, set it in the configuration file or with the config argument of runperftests or PerfTest.transform instead) and invalid TOML are reported as parse errors, so no suite is generated. The valid keys of the macro still apply to the rest of the transform, so its other errors are reported as well.\n\nInterpolation:\n\nValues can be interpolated inside TOML strings, e.g. dedicated_reference_file = 'ref_$(gethostname()).JLD2'. Those keys take their value when the generated suite is executed. An interpolated value is always a string, so only keys that take a String can be interpolated.\n\nArguments\n\nA string literal, with the TOML declaration of configuration keys. It is read when the recipe is transformed, so a variable or any other expression is reported as a parse error.\n\nExample\n\n@perftestconfig \" [roofline]   enabled = false [general]   maxsaved_results = 1   recursive = false \"\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"macros/#PerfTest.@on_perftest_exec","page":"Macros","title":"PerfTest.@on_perftest_exec","text":"The expression given to this macro will only be executed in the generated suite, and will be deleted if the source code is executed as is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"macros/#PerfTest.@on_perftest_ignore","page":"Macros","title":"PerfTest.@on_perftest_ignore","text":"The expression given to this macro will only be executed in the source code, and will be deleted in the generated performance test suite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"macros/#PerfTest.@export_vars","page":"Macros","title":"PerfTest.@export_vars","text":"@export_vars vars...\n\nExports the specified symbols –along with the values they hold at the moment of the calling– to the scope of metric definitions. In order to use any variable on the definition of a metric such variable needs to be exported with this macro.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"examples/memcopyCellArray3D_ParallelStencil/#Roofline-example:-option-pricing","page":"Roofline example: option pricing","title":"Roofline example: option pricing","text":"In this example we setup a roofline model methodology to test the resolution of a binomial tree using a slow implementation.\n\nrfd_rt(r,t_per_p) = rfd(t) = exp(-r * t * t_per_p)\nup_st(sigma, t_per_p) = exp(sigma * sqrt(t_per_p))\n\nstruct _Parameters\n    sigma   :: Float64\n    periods :: Int\n    t_per_p :: Float64\n    rfd     :: Function\n    up      :: Float64\n    K       :: Float64\n    S0      :: Float64\n    p       :: Float64\nend\n\nfunction setupProblem(sigma, t, periods_per_t, risk_free_rate, strike, price, p) :: _Parameters\n    return _Parameters(\n        sigma,\n        t * periods_per_t,\n        1 / periods_per_t,\n        rfd_rt(risk_free_rate, 1 / periods_per_t),\n        up_st(sigma, 1 / periods_per_t),\n        strike,\n        price,\n        p\n    )\nend\n\nfunction createBinomialTree(p :: _Parameters)::Tuple{Matrix,Array}\n\n    nodes = (p.periods + 1) * p.periods ÷ 2\n    # A and b\n    Tree = zeros(Float64, nodes, nodes)\n    B = zeros(Float64, nodes)\n\n    # Fill the body of the tree with the discounted expectations\n    for period in 1:(p.periods-1)\n        _nodes = ((period-1)*period÷2+1):((period+1)*(period+0)÷2)\n        for node in _nodes\n            Tree[node, node] = -1\n            Tree[node, node+period] = p.p * p.rfd(p.periods - period)\n            Tree[node, node+period+1] = 1 - p.p * p.rfd(p.periods - period)\n        end\n    end\n\n    # Fill the last level of the tree with the prices at maturity\n    xa = ((p.periods - 1) * p.periods ÷ 2 + 1)\n    xb = ((p.periods + 1) * (p.periods + 0) ÷ 2)\n    down = 1 / p.up\n\n    for node in xa:xb\n        Tree[node, node] = 1\n        B[node] = max((p.S0 * p.up^(xb - node) * down^(node - xa)) - p.K, 0.0)\n    end\n    return Tree, B\nend\n\n\nfunction solveTree(A::Matrix, b::Array, periods::Int)::Array\n\n    nodes = (periods + 1) * periods ÷ 2\n    X = zeros(Float64, nodes)\n    # Last level nodes from a to b\n    xa = ((periods - 1) * periods ÷ 2 + 1)\n    xb = ((periods + 1) * (periods + 0) ÷ 2)\n    for node in xa:xb\n        X[node] = b[node]\n    end\n    # Tree core nodes\n    for period in (periods-1):-1:1\n        _nodes = ((period-1)*period÷2+1):((period+1)*(period+0)÷2)\n        for node in _nodes\n            X[node] = A[node, node+period] * b[node+period] + A[node, node+period+1] * b[node+period+1]\n            b[node] = X[node]\n        end\n    end\n\n    return X\nend\n\noption_price(solution :: Array) = solution[1]\n\n\nThe following is the recipe file that sets up the performance test suite:\n\nusing Test\nusing PerfTest\n\ninclude(\"main3.jl\")\n\n# CONFIG\n@perftest_config begin\n    regression.enabled = false\n    roofline.autoflops = true\n    roofline.tolerance.min_percentage = 1.0\nend\n\n\n@testset \"Performance Tests\" begin\n\n    @testset \"Tree construction\" begin\n\n        @testset \"Periods\" for n_periods in [16, 64, 128]\n\n            param = setupProblem(0.04, 1, n_periods, 0.04, 100, 100, 0.5)\n\n            @roofline actual_flops=:autoflop target_ratio=0.05 begin\n                mem = ((:iterator + 1) * :iterator)\n                :autoflop / mem\n            end\n            A, b = createBinomialTree(param)\n\n\n            @perftest solveTree(A, b, param.periods)\n        end\n    end\nend\n\nThe recipe file specifies the following:\n\nThe target will be evaluated using a roofline model\nThe basic regression methodology is disabled\nThe tolerance below the threshold will be zero\nThe autoflops feature is enabled\nThe roofline macro additionally receives how to calculate the flop count (actual_lops=:autoflop) and the threshold of the actual performance vs model performance is 5%","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/mock2-memorythroughput/#Effective-memory-throughput","page":"Effective memory throughput","title":"Effective memory throughput","text":"This mock example explains how to setup the effective memory throughput methodology.\n\nThe memory bandwidth test is more appropriate for functions that are primarily moving data, with reduced protagonism to computation performance.\n\n\nusing Test\nusing PerfTest\n\n\n@perftest_config \"\n[regression]\nenabled = false\n\"\n\n\n# TEST TARGET\nfunction copyvec_squared(a :: Vector{Int})\n\n    b = zeros(length(a))\n\n    for i in 1:length(a)\n        b[i] = a[i] * a[i]\n    end\n\n    return b\nend\n\n@testset \"Example\" begin\n\n    N = 1000000\n\n    a = rand(Int, N)\n\n    # The ratio sets the threshold, being 1.0 the maximum empirical bandwidth and 0.6 = 60% of such maximum\n    @define_eff_memory_throughput ratio=0.6 begin\n        # The main block of the macro holds the formula for the bandwidth (therefore BYTES divided by SECONDS) on\n        # one single execution of the test target.\n        # In this case per execution N elements of 4 Bytes are Read on Memort + Written\n        # on Cache + Written on Memory (Copy on write assumption by default)\n        # The median time is considered an adequate measure for the denominator in this case\n        # THUS:\n        N * 4 * 3 / :median_time\n    end\n\n    # Set the target\n    b = @perftest copyvec_squared(a)\nend\n\nThis test results in a failure to meet the expectation.","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration_params/#Configuration-Parameters","page":"Configuration","title":"Configuration Parameters","text":"This document describes all configuration parameters available. Parameters are organized by section, they are specified in TOML format.\n\nEvery transformation starts from the default values below, overridden by the perftest_config.toml file in the working directory. If the file does not exist it is created with the default values. The file does not need to define every parameter: missing sections or parameters keep their default value, and a parameter with an incorrect type is reported as an error and keeps its default value as well. A section or parameter that does not exist (e.g. a misspelled one) is reported as an error, naming it along with the existing sections, or the existing parameters of its section, and is ignored while the rest of the file applies. A file that cannot be parsed is reported as an error and left untouched, and the default configuration is used instead.\n\nThe configuration is used when a recipe is transformed, and the generated suite keeps the configuration it was generated with: when it executes, wherever and however that happens, it uses those same values. The only exception is the [bencher] section, see below. Values that depend on the machine are fixed at generation as well, but the ones that describe it relatively (e.g. a thread arrangement like :numa, or a buffer size of 0) are resolved on the machine that executes the suite. A @perftest_config macro applies from its place in the recipe onwards. In an included recipe (see recursive) it applies only to the part of the suite generated from that recipe, the including recipe gets its own configuration back after it.\n\nThe argument of @perftest_config must be a string literal, as it is read when the recipe is transformed (a variable or any other expression is reported as a parse error). Its content is checked against the parameters below: invalid TOML, a section or parameter that does not exist (naming the existing ones, as for the file), a parameter with an incorrect type (naming the expected and the given type) and a parameter that is used before the recipe is read ([MPI] enabled) are reported as parse errors, so the suite is not generated. The rest of its parameters still apply during the transform, so the errors of the rest of the recipe are reported as well.\n\nThe @perftest_config string may interpolate values inside TOML strings, e.g. dedicated_reference_file = 'ref_$(gethostname()).JLD2'. Those keys take their value when the generated suite executes, during the transform they keep their previous value while the rest of the keys are applied as usual. An interpolated value is therefore always a string, so interpolation can only set parameters that take a String (e.g. not verbose = '$(level)'), otherwise it is reported as a parse error. Interpolation anywhere else (as a whole value, in a key or in a table name) is reported as a parse error as well.\n\nThe parameters given with the config keyword argument of runperftests or PerfTest.transform take priority over the file, and are checked one by one: one with an incorrect type is reported as an error and ignored, the rest apply. A section or parameter that does not exist is reported as an error as well, naming the existing ones as for the file, and ignored. The same type check happens when a generated suite executes, in case it was generated by a version of PerfTest with different parameters.\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration_params/#[general]","page":"Configuration","title":"[general]","text":"General settings that control the overall behavior of the package.\n\nParameter Type Default Description\nautoflops Bool false Deprecated: automatic flop counting is outdated and not supported (the :autoflop primitive is always 0). While it is true, a @roofline without actual_flops is reported as a parse error: give the flop count of the target to @roofline with actual_flops instead. Setting the key alone is not an error. A formula or a @regression metric that uses :autoflop is reported as a parse error regardless of this key.\nnumas String | Integer | Float64 \"single\" Amount of NUMA domains whose threads the suite uses, along with threads_per_numa. Together they are the thread setting of the suite, which runperftests launches it with: it sets the number of threads of the process that executes the suite (--threads), it does not pin them. A string names a whole arrangement and is given in both keys: \"single\" (1 thread), \"numa\" (every thread of 1 NUMA domain) or \"all\" (every thread of every NUMA domain). Otherwise both are numbers, each one an integer n ≥ 1 or a float in (0, 1], the ratio of the available ones (e.g. numas = 1 and threads_per_numa = 0.5 is half of the threads of 1 NUMA domain). Anything else, e.g. two different names or a name along with a number, is reported as an error, naming the reason and where each key comes from: a parse error if a @perftest_config sets one of them. The arrangement is resolved on the machine that executes the suite. A suite has a single thread setting: each key takes its value from the last @perftest_config that sets it, even when it is in an included recipe, and a @perftest_threads in the recipe takes priority over both.\nthreads_per_numa String | Integer | Float64 \"single\" Amount of threads to use per NUMA domain, see numas.\nsave_results Bool true Whether to record the results of executing the performance suite. If false, an execution writes neither the result file (see save_folder) nor the [regression] dedicated_reference_file, the existing ones are still used as regression reference.\nlogs_enabled Bool true Enable or disable the log subsystem. If false, nothing is logged from where it is set (neither kept in memory nor printed by verbose), and no log folder nor log file is written: the transform saves its logs (see verbose) only if it is true in the configuration in force at the end of the recipe. The generated suite writes no log file.\nsave_folder String \".perftests\" Folder where performance suite results shall be stored, the suite creates it when it saves its results.\nmax_saved_results Int 20 Maximum amount of performance test suite execution results to be saved in the result file for each suite.\nplotting Bool true Whether to have plots in the test output of methodologies that support it.\nverbose Int 0 Whether to output the collected logs in the standard output. From 2 on, the logs of the transform are saved in the .perftest_logs folder, as decided by the value in force at the end of the recipe (e.g. a @perftest_config of the recipe that sets it).\nrecursive Bool true If enabled, whenever a file is included inside a recipe that is being transformed, it will be transformed as well.\nsafe_formulas Bool false Deprecated.\nsuppress_output Bool true Whether to hide the output of the test targets. If false the output of each execution will be shown, which will be probably a long output.\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration_params/#[regression]","page":"Configuration","title":"[regression]","text":"Settings for regression testing.\n\nParameter Type Default Description\nenabled Bool true Whether to use regression testing in this suite.\ndedicated_reference_file String \"\" If not an empty string, a succesful suite execution will be saved into this file, as well as in the (result file see [general] save_folder). The last saved execution will be used as reference for regression testing. If empty the package will look at the result file instead, and find the latest sucessful execution as reference.\ndefault_threshold Number 1.1 The threshold of a @regression that gives none, and of the regression every top level testset gets. A metric with low_is_bad = false (e.g. a time, the default regression) takes this value, its ratio new/reference must stay below it. A metric with low_is_bad = true (the default of @regression, e.g. a throughput) takes 2 − this value, its ratio must stay above it: 1.1 tolerates a time up to 10% higher and a throughput up to 10% lower (0.9). A value of 2 or more leaves no threshold for a metric with low_is_bad = true, such a @regression is reported as a parse error, give it an explicit threshold.\nuse_bencher Bool false Whether to enable Bencher to upload results to an online CI/CD performance benchmark platform. THIS FEATURE IS EXPERIMENTAL and likely unstable.\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration_params/#[roofline]","page":"Configuration","title":"[roofline]","text":"Settings for roofline analysis.\n\nParameter Type Default Description\nenabled Bool true Whether to use roofline methodologies in this suite.\ndefault_threshold Number 0.5 Default threshold of the @roofline macro if no threshold is specified.\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration_params/#[memory_bandwidth]","page":"Configuration","title":"[memory_bandwidth]","text":"Settings for memory bandwidth analysis.\n\nParameter Type Default Description\nenabled Bool true Whether to use effective memory throughput methodology in this suite.\ndefault_threshold Number 0.5 Default thresholds of the @define_eff_mem_throughput macro if no threshold is specified.\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration_params/#[perfcompare]","page":"Configuration","title":"[perfcompare]","text":"Settings for performance comparison.\n\nParameter Type Default Description\nenabled Bool true Whether to use the @perfcompare macro in this suite.\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration_params/#[machine_benchmarking]","page":"Configuration","title":"[machine_benchmarking]","text":"Settings for benchmarking the underlying machine.\n\nParameter Type Default Description\nmemory_bandwidth_test_buffer_size Int 0 If 0 a buffer size for bandwith benchmarks is used so its at least 4 times bigger than the biggest cache level of the machine that executes the suite. If non-zero this value is used to set the buffer size instead.\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration_params/#[MPI]","page":"Configuration","title":"[MPI]","text":"Settings for MPI-based execution.\n\nParameter Type Default Description\nenabled Bool false Whether to enable the MPI aware performance suite generation, this will mainly make sure tests are measured in all ranks but evaluated on the main rank only, and that machine benchmarks take into account all ranks. The MPI mode is decided before the recipe is read, so it can only be set in the perftest_config.toml file or with the config argument of runperftests or PerfTest.transform, a @perftest_config that sets it is reported as a parse error.\nmode String \"reduce\" This has no function as of now, but it will be used in future versions of perftest.\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"configuration_params/#[bencher]","page":"Configuration","title":"[bencher]","text":"Settings for Bencher integration. This feature is experimental, and likely prone to bugs.\n\nUnlike the rest of the configuration, this section is read when the suite executes, from the perftest_config.toml file of the working directory (the default values are used if there is none), so the credentials are never written into a generated suite. A @perftest_config in the recipe that sets these keys takes priority over the file, but its values are written into the suite: interpolate secrets to keep them out of it, e.g. api_key = '$(ENV[\"BENCHER_API_KEY\"])'.\n\nParameter Type Default Description\napi_key String \"\" The Bencher platform key to use to connect to bencher.\napi_url String \"https://api.bencher.dev\" The API url.\nproject_name String \"\" The name of the project where metrics and testbeds shall be posted.\norganization String \"\" The organization that holds the project.\ncustom_testbed_name String \"\" The customized identification of this machine (a.k.a testbed for Bencher) when uploading posting suite execution results.","category":"section"},{"location":"#[PerfTest.jl](https://github.com/JuliaPerf/PerfTest.jl)-[![Star-on-GitHub](https://img.shields.io/github/stars/JuliaPerf/PerfTest.jl.svg)](https://github.com/JuliaPerf/PerfTest.jl/stargazers)","page":"Introduction","title":"PerfTest.jl (Image: Star on GitHub)","text":"The package PerfTest provides the user with a performance regression unit testing framework. This framework consists of a collection of macros used to declaratively define a performance test suite. Scripts with said macros can then be transformed into performance suites using the transform method. This package is focused on providing an easy and fast way to develop performance suites, with additional features to customise them following the demands of the use case.","category":"section"},{"location":"#Dependencies","page":"Introduction","title":"Dependencies","text":"PerfTest relies on:\n\nBenchmarkTools\nBandwidthBenchmark\nConfigurations\nDates\nHwloc\nHTTP\nJLD2\nJSON\nLibGit2\nLinearAlgebra\nMLStyle\nMacroTools\nPkg\nPrecompileTools\nPrintf\nRevise\nSuppressor\nTOML\nTest\nUnicodePlots","category":"section"},{"location":"#Contributors","page":"Introduction","title":"Contributors","text":"This project has been developed as a Master's thesis by Daniel Sergio Vega Rodriguez. Thus, the contributors to this project have been so far:\n\nDaniel Sergio Vega Rodriguez (@Dvegrod), Università della Svizzera italiana (USI): package developer\nDr. Samuel Omlin (@omlins), Swiss National Supercomputing Centre (CSCS), ETH Zurich: original idea and supervisor\nProf. Olaf Schenk, Università della Svizzera italiana (USI): PhD supervisor\nDr. Pasadakis Dimosthenis, Università della Svizzera italiana (USI): additional advisor","category":"section"}]
}
